next lecture will be inference, so you're

23
00:01:03,070 --> 00:01:04,129
going to go back and forth.

24
00:01:04,129 --> 00:01:06,712
You're going to do scaling laws and inference and then scaling

25
00:01:06,712 --> 00:01:09,150
laws again.

26
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:13,970
So scaling laws is really the following scenario in some ways,

27
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:17,050
or you run into it very quickly once you run into this scenario.

28
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:19,830
So you have your very wealthy friend,

29
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:22,870
who has given you 10,000 B200s for a month,

30
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:25,950
and he or she has asked you to build a very good open source

31
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:26,650
language model.

32
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,070
They would like to get something out of those B200s.

33
00:01:30,070 --> 00:01:32,450
And so you've already put together your infra team.

34
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:35,830
That's your assignment to buddies.

35
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:38,295
And you have, let's say a good pre-training data set.

36
00:01:38,295 --> 00:01:40,170
That's going to be the assignment after this,

37
00:01:40,170 --> 00:01:42,070
but let's say you already have that.

38
00:01:42,070 --> 00:01:44,370
And then you're going to go and train a big model,

39
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:47,430
but there's lots of choices in training a big model— all

40
00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:50,830
the assignments, the earlier lecture two stuff that we had

41
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:54,350
earlier, where you have questions like what architecture

42
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:54,930
should I use?

43
00:01:54,930 --> 00:01:56,730
What should my hyperparameters be?

44
00:01:56,730 --> 00:01:58,580
And it's very scary to run these things

45
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,540
on a run that is basically, potentially

46
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,700
millions of dollars in cost, so how

47
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:06,400
do you really take this idea of scaling up very seriously?

48
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,940
How do you make sure that your big run is actually successful?

49
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,460
So of these many choices, some of which

50
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,605
you're going to just copy out of the literature.

51
00:02:15,605 --> 00:02:17,980
The stuff that I talked about earlier in the architecture

52
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:23,120
lecture is really about well-adopted best practices,

53
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:26,080
and you will probably just pick some of these out of a hat.

54
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,520
That's fine, but really, let's say you're at the frontier.

55
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,140
You would like to build something

56
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:34,460
that's actually better than the best available models today.

57
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,420
You have to start really optimizing these.

58
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,700
You can't just copy the choices of others

59
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:41,620
and get something that's better than the state of the art.

60
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:42,880
So how do we do that?

61
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:45,340
Well, that's going to require us to really engage

62
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:48,220
with this idea of scaling.

63
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:52,260
So scaling laws have been one very powerful tool.

64
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:53,980
You might also call it a paradigm.

65
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:57,020
Scaling laws, if you talk to some people who are really

66
00:02:57,020 --> 00:02:59,080
in these big labs doing scaling work,

67
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:00,593
it's almost kind of a way of life.

68
00:03:00,593 --> 00:03:02,760
They're like, we really believe in the scaling laws.

69
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,420
It's almost a belief, and you'll see

70
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:08,820
why scaling laws can sometimes be quite tricky objects.

71
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:11,700
But really, scaling laws are these simple, predictive rules

72
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:16,200
of how to go from small scale model performance and behavior,

73
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,440
and to try to extrapolate them up to large scale behavior.

74
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:23,620
And so the basic naive way to approach model training

75
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:25,480
might be to say, I have a big budget,

76
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,460
I'm going to do multiple training runs on these very

77
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:30,460
big runs, and I'm going to tune hyperparameters

78
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:33,260
on those big runs, but that's very wasteful.

79
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:34,740
So instead, what I would like to do

80
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:38,180
is I would like to do all of my optimization at the small scale

81
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:40,100
and have some simple rule that allows

82
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:43,140
me to extrapolate small scale behaviors to large scale

83
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:43,960
behaviors.

84
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:45,940
And if the small scale and large scale

85
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,300
are connected by some very simple, robust connections

86
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:52,100
like regularities of some kind, then you

87
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:55,410
would have confidence in this kind of new scaling approach

88
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:57,170
to optimizing your system.

89
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:58,810
And that's really what today is all

90
00:03:58,810 --> 00:04:04,790
going to be about-- this engineering view of a scaling law.

91
00:04:04,790 --> 00:04:07,890
And to start with, I'm going to talk a little bit

92
00:04:07,890 --> 00:04:10,490
about background, because scaling laws are very

93
00:04:10,490 --> 00:04:14,570
interesting in that not only are they the newest paradigm, that's

94
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:17,329
how a lot of these models are designed and thought,

95
00:04:17,329 --> 00:04:21,470
but also they connect very closely to very classical ideas.

96
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:25,923
So if you're a person that's a machine learning theorist,

97
00:04:25,923 --> 00:04:28,090
actually, there are some things that you will really

98
00:04:28,090 --> 00:04:30,970
feel at home with in some ways, so they're like empirical sample

99
00:04:30,970 --> 00:04:32,090
complexities.

100
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:35,730
And then I'll talk through the history of scaling laws of one

101
00:04:35,730 --> 00:04:38,365
kind and show you that this is not something that's really new.

102
00:04:38,365 --> 00:04:40,490
It's something with actually quite a bit of history

103
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:44,090
and research done to it.

104
00:04:44,090 --> 00:04:47,270
So I really, really had to put some math into my slides.

105
00:04:47,270 --> 00:04:50,690
I really feel the need to do so, but one

106
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:52,530
of the things that is very interesting

107
00:04:52,530 --> 00:04:55,320
is that for the longest time, machine learning

108
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:58,600
has thought about this question of how will my model perform,

109
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:00,300
especially from a theory perspective?

110
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:04,280
I have this model class, how good is my model going to be?

111
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:06,840
And generalization bound is the theorists answer

112
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:09,400
to this question, so you have your error,

113
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,000
over some finite hypothesis class,

114
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:12,417
and you say, oh, my error is going

115
00:05:12,417 --> 00:05:15,600
to be at most this much worse than the model's

116
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,040
performance on my training set.

117
00:05:17,040 --> 00:05:20,000
This is classic generalization bounds.

118
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,240
You can write this down for more complex classes

119
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,320
like smooth densities or whatever,

120
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,280
but importantly, not only does this

121
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:28,480
tell you some of performance, notice

122
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:31,880
that these bounds are often dependent on the sample size.

123
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:36,720
And so this is telling you, well, this a theoretical upper bound on my loss values

124
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:39,220
as a function of how my training set grows.

125
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:42,400
And in some ways, that's very closely related to this idea

126
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:45,860
of what happens as I scale up the amount of data that I have?

127
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:47,760
Those are closely related questions,

128
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:51,590
where you might believe me that that's true.

129
00:05:51,590 --> 00:05:55,030
And if you try to figure out what is the very first scaling

130
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:56,410
law paper that exists?

131
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:58,950
It turns out you can go back quite far.

132
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:02,750
You can go back to Bell Labs and Corinna Cortes and Vladimir

133
00:06:02,750 --> 00:06:06,750
Vapnik and these folks that were doing very serious theory

134
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:10,550
work on machine learning, ask this question, well,

135
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:12,990
training classifiers on a really large data set

136
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:14,650
is often very expensive.

137
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:16,530
We would like to avoid doing that,

138
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:19,870
and to do so maybe we can just fit classifiers on a smaller

139
00:06:19,870 --> 00:06:23,390
sample, fit a curve to how their error rates decay,

140
00:06:23,390 --> 00:06:25,630
and use that as a way to estimate their performance.

141
00:06:25,630 --> 00:06:29,990
That's almost literally a data scaling law back in 1993.

142
00:06:29,990 --> 00:06:33,190
So this idea, at the very least, thinking

143
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:34,710
about how your model's performance

144
00:06:34,710 --> 00:06:37,430
changes as a function of data set size is just a very

145
00:06:37,430 --> 00:06:40,310
classical one, and there's a lot of work that

146
00:06:40,310 --> 00:06:41,970
supports this kind of idea.

147
00:06:41,970 --> 00:06:44,650
I just want to connect this to some of these older ideas

148
00:06:44,650 --> 00:06:47,950
so you don't really think of scaling laws as this very

149
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,590
new, purely neural thing.

150
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:53,070
It's an idea that has a lot of roots in empirical machine

151
00:06:53,070 --> 00:06:56,790
learning, and also connections to theoretical machine learning.

152
00:06:56,790 --> 00:06:59,550
And if you start to look at the history of this,

153
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:03,150
there's a lot of people that have said, isn't it the case

154
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:06,290
that maybe instead of spending money on algorithm development,

155
00:07:06,290 --> 00:07:08,370
maybe we should just go collect more data,

156
00:07:08,370 --> 00:07:11,830
because if we plot how the performance of our systems

157
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:14,390
behave as a function of the amount of data that we have,

158
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:17,670
it turns out that they are all increasing or improving

159
00:07:17,670 --> 00:07:18,610
in predictable ways?

160
00:07:18,610 --> 00:07:24,750
Banko and Brill, is one of the canonical NLP references in how

161
00:07:24,750 --> 00:07:27,550
the amount of data and thinking about scaling

162
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:30,790
is a really valid way of improving system performance,

163
00:07:30,790 --> 00:07:34,510
so there's lots of ways of thinking about this.

164
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:38,750
And there's also work by other NLP folks

165
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:40,450
like Kolachina et al. in 2012.

166
00:07:40,450 --> 00:07:42,630
This is one of the earlier works on different kinds

167
00:07:42,630 --> 00:07:45,390
of functional forms for scaling laws, where once again, they're

168
00:07:45,390 --> 00:07:47,870
increasing the amount of data that they're using to train.

169
00:07:47,870 --> 00:07:51,380
In this case, machine translation systems or BLEU scores.

170
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:54,500
And they're asking like, what is the right functional form

171
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:57,060
for how a model's performance behaves

172
00:07:57,060 --> 00:07:59,680
as a function of increased data amount?

173
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:02,960
And they maybe surprisingly or not surprisingly,

174
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:07,660
end up with the same power laws, which is pow 3 and 4 as really

175
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:08,400
we do today.

176
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,020
For the rest of the lecture, you're

177
00:08:10,020 --> 00:08:14,060
going to keep seeing essentially these polynomial functions, pow

178
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:17,080
3 and pow 4 as I talk about scaling laws.

179
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:21,260
And we knew about a lot of this back in the 2010s

180
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:23,380
that these kinds of functional forms

181
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:27,940
were very predictive of model behavior as we scaled them up.

182
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:31,820
And then finally, I really like mentioning this paper because

183
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:33,700
for whatever reason, they don't get,

184
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:35,179
or Hestness et al. don't get cited

185
00:08:35,179 --> 00:08:37,020
quite as much as they should.

186
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:40,500
The origins of neural scaling, I would point back

187
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:42,580
to Hestness in 2017.

188
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:44,820
Really a very forward looking work

189
00:08:44,820 --> 00:08:47,530
in many ways that built these scaling

190
00:08:47,530 --> 00:08:49,570
laws as a function of data set size

191
00:08:49,570 --> 00:08:52,370
for a wide variety of systems, different kinds of speech

192
00:08:52,370 --> 00:08:55,010
recognition systems, machine translation systems, language

193
00:08:55,010 --> 00:08:58,530
models, and they showed that these things follow

194
00:08:58,530 --> 00:09:02,290
these nice polynomial trends across a really large number

195
00:09:02,290 --> 00:09:04,330
of domains.

196
00:09:04,330 --> 00:09:05,930
And it was really ahead of its time,

197
00:09:05,930 --> 00:09:09,970
because 2017 was long before some of the earlier OpenAI

198
00:09:09,970 --> 00:09:12,890
scaling laws and so on in the 2020s.

199
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:15,230
And they talked about stuff that we talk about today,

200
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:18,250
like emergence, where suddenly models

201
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:19,970
可能会展现出某些类型的能力

202
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:22,530
因为准确率是一个更加不连续的度量

203
00:09:22,530 --> 00:09:24,250
而不是损失。

204
00:09:24,250 --> 00:09:27,490
而实际上，按计算量进行缩放，如果我们有系统

205
00:09:27,490 --> 00:09:30,650
能够可预测地扩展到大规模训练数据，那么

206
00:09:30,650 --> 00:09:32,950
计算量确实会变得非常重要。

207
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:36,330
最后，既然我们按计算量进行缩放，那么

208
00:09:36,330 --> 00:09:39,390
速度和系统优化将会转化为准确率。

209
00:09:39,390 --> 00:09:42,070
所以在某些方面，我们今天看到的很多事情，

210
00:09:42,070 --> 00:09:45,240
比如系统缩放的现象等等，

211
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:48,440
我们本可以知道，或者我在2017年本可以知道，

212
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:52,280
如果我当时阅读并思考了这些缩放定律论文

213
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:54,360
并更认真地思考它们。

214
00:09:54,360 --> 00:10:00,200
所以在我讲的每一场缩放定律

215
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:03,760
讲座中都要提到那篇论文，只是为了说明，很多这些现象

216
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:06,080
在现代时代之前就早已为人所知，

217
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:10,080
或者说在现代语言模型时代之前，因为

218
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:12,280
我们有可能在见到这些大型语言模型之前

219
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:14,960
就已经知道我们所处的状态。

220
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:17,160
知道这一点非常酷。

221
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:20,420
关于这段半历史性的部分有什么问题吗？

222
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:21,260
是的。

223
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:28,120
[听不清]，我想知道那些只是纯粹经验上的，还是有什么方法

224
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:30,200
可以证明这一切的合理性？

225
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:32,160
你问的是这些缩放定律，

226
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:33,680
它们从何而来？

227
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:37,760
这些是纯粹的曲线拟合练习。

228
00:10:37,760 --> 00:10:40,960
我会稍微谈一下缩放定律可能从何而来，

229
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:45,120
以及为什么多项式可以说是非常自然的一类缩放

230
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:49,980
定律，但并没有金科玉律说它们只能是这些。

231
00:10:49,980 --> 00:10:52,620
不过我会说，在某些方面，

232
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:55,680
理论提供了一个非常丰富的地方，缩放定律的

233
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,240
函数形式可以从中而来，因为理论

234
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:01,360
经常在思考误差率如何衰减

235
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:02,860
作为各种对象的函数？

236
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:04,693
它们会给你各种类型的

237
00:11:04,693 --> 00:11:05,460
候选函数形式。

238
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:06,640
物理学家也非常擅长这个

239
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:07,670
因为他们思考极限，

240
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:09,340
比如极限如何表现？

241
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:12,980
And that gives you different scaling law functional forms.

242
00:11:12,980 --> 00:11:13,480
Cool.

243
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:21,560
All right, any other questions?

244
00:11:21,560 --> 00:11:22,840
Good.

245
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:26,320
So now I'm going to start talking about more modern neural

246
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:28,440
language model scaling, and I'm going

247
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:31,480
to start with data scaling laws because to me, they're the most

248
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:34,320
simple and natural objects.

249
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:35,960
Talking about data scaling laws also

250
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:39,760
allows me to walk you through very step-by-step on how

251
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,990
we might guess what the functional form of a scaling law

252
00:11:43,990 --> 00:11:47,150
might be and why the polynomial form is a natural one.

253
00:11:47,150 --> 00:11:49,670
And then I'm going to start talking about hyperparameters

254
00:11:49,670 --> 00:11:53,070
and architectures and all sorts of other more exotic objects

255
00:11:53,070 --> 00:11:55,730
that are going to be harder to justify from intuition,

256
00:11:55,730 --> 00:12:00,910
but still turn out to have very nice scaling law behaviors.

257
00:12:00,910 --> 00:12:04,310
One of the things that's remarkable about this scaling

258
00:12:04,310 --> 00:12:07,950
law style analysis has been that these power law relationships

259
00:12:07,950 --> 00:12:12,510
seem to hold for a huge variety of different factors.

260
00:12:12,510 --> 00:12:14,970
So the basic ones that we see in pre-training,

261
00:12:14,970 --> 00:12:16,762
and those are going to be the ones that I'm

262
00:12:16,762 --> 00:12:18,230
going to talk about today.

263
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:21,677
The usual ones that you see are compute on the x-axis.

264
00:12:21,677 --> 00:12:22,510
This is log compute.

265
00:12:22,510 --> 00:12:25,750
Sorry, on the x-axis, log of test loss on the y-axis,

266
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:28,050
or log of the data set on x-axis,

267
00:12:28,050 --> 00:12:30,550
log of loss on the y-axis, log of parameters

268
00:12:30,550 --> 00:12:33,610
on x-axis, log of loss, once again, on the y-axis.

269
00:12:33,610 --> 00:12:36,150
So we can get a variety of different x-axes

270
00:12:36,150 --> 00:12:39,130
that you might think of as resources in the log axis.

271
00:12:39,130 --> 00:12:43,100
All of them linearize log test loss here.

272
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:47,000
You can see also much more exotic scaling laws.

273
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,900
You can see scaling laws that are basically

274
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:53,280
the equivalent to these in downstream benchmarks.

275
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:56,340
In those cases, you expect to see a sigmoid functional form

276
00:12:56,340 --> 00:12:59,740
as a function of compute or compute-like quantities.

277
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:02,340
There's even been many works recently, more

278
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:04,700
on the forecasting side, where people are doing things

279
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:07,440
like putting dates as the x-axis.

280
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:09,140
And strangely enough, at least for these

281
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:11,540
plots—like the upper envelope of these curves

282
00:13:11,540 --> 00:13:16,220
are often linear in various forms of capabilities.

283
00:13:16,220 --> 00:13:18,880
There's no reason that on this bottom side,

284
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:21,980
the more exotic scaling laws need to be true.

285
00:13:21,980 --> 00:13:25,540
But it turns out to be the case that, I guess, language model

286
00:13:25,540 --> 00:13:28,620
performance is often much more regular as a function of scale

287
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:33,860
and resources than it initially might appear.

288
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:37,200
So to begin with, I'm going to start with data scaling laws.

289
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,650
And data scaling laws are a very simple kind

290
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:42,530
of univariate relationship that we're going to think about.

291
00:13:42,530 --> 00:13:45,050
So in this world, we're going to fix our model training

292
00:13:45,050 --> 00:13:46,010
procedure.

293
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:48,070
And in general, unless I say otherwise,

294
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:49,570
the models are going to generally be

295
00:13:49,570 --> 00:13:51,530
bigger than the data set size.

296
00:13:51,530 --> 00:13:55,190
And I'm going to increase my data set size in a regular way,

297
00:13:55,190 --> 00:13:57,390
and I'm going to see how the error reduces.

298
00:13:57,390 --> 00:13:59,690
And what do we expect out of this relationship?

299
00:13:59,690 --> 00:14:02,250
In general, it's going to be monotone-ish

300
00:14:02,250 --> 00:14:03,990
if we tune our hyperparameters well.

301
00:14:03,990 --> 00:14:05,970
Because we have more data, more data

302
00:14:05,970 --> 00:14:08,550
should help us do better at our task.

303
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,050
The error should decrease.

304
00:14:10,050 --> 00:14:12,570
And if you're doing something like classification, or even

305
00:14:12,570 --> 00:14:14,810
for next token prediction, you should really

306
00:14:14,810 --> 00:14:18,490
go from random guessing to some entropy-like object

307
00:14:18,490 --> 00:14:21,690
where you can't do any better than the irreducible error

308
00:14:21,690 --> 00:14:22,350
of your task.

309
00:14:22,350 --> 00:14:23,350
That's the noise floor.

310
00:14:23,350 --> 00:14:26,970
So we're going to have some sigmoidal-like object over here.

311
00:14:26,970 --> 00:14:31,290
So this is generally what we expect out of our system.

312
00:14:31,290 --> 00:14:34,630
So we're going to start with an empirical observation,

313
00:14:34,630 --> 00:14:38,510
and should make this on your own.

314
00:14:38,510 --> 00:14:40,370
You'll make something that looks roughly

315
00:14:40,370 --> 00:14:43,610
like this in parts of assignment 3,

316
00:14:43,610 --> 00:14:46,410
which is if we just have a very big model,

317
00:14:46,410 --> 00:14:48,650
much bigger than our data set, and we

318
00:14:48,650 --> 00:14:51,530
increase the data set size, and we put that on the log axis,

319
00:14:51,530 --> 00:14:54,230
and on the y-axis, we have log test loss.

320
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:59,050
Once again, you will find that the two points that you plot

321
00:14:59,050 --> 00:15:01,790
会形成一条非常清晰的线性趋势。

322
00:15:01,790 --> 00:15:04,890
我从 Kaplan 2020 年的论文中取了这个。

323
00:15:04,890 --> 00:15:06,870
我会反复引用这篇论文。

324
00:15:06,870 --> 00:15:09,970
这是 OpenAI 的《神经缩放定律》论文。

325
00:15:09,970 --> 00:15:13,930
他们做了很多漂亮的消融实验和大量漂亮的数据。

326
00:15:13,930 --> 00:15:16,610
现在，这个关系，我

327
00:15:16,610 --> 00:15:18,910
在对数-对数图上画了一条线，

328
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:23,090
这是一个无尺度或幂律关系。

329
00:15:23,090 --> 00:15:24,110
那这意味着什么？

330
00:15:24,110 --> 00:15:26,258
如果我在对数-对数图上有一条线？

331
00:15:26,258 --> 00:15:28,550
对很多人来说，这可能是显而易见的。

332
00:15:28,550 --> 00:15:30,210
但为了真正强调这一点，

333
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:32,130
如果某个东西在对数-对数图上是线性的，那

334
00:15:32,130 --> 00:15:33,050
意味着什么？

335
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:35,530
这意味着我有的误差

336
00:15:35,530 --> 00:15:37,280
是多项式衰减的。

337
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:39,400
而且，这通常意味着我离

338
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:40,340
我的渐近线还很远。

339
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:41,940
因为一旦我接近我的渐近线，

340
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:45,280
我会逐渐变缓，而不是保持一条直线。

341
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:48,320
所以让我们看一个例子，只是为了向你展示

342
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:51,880
缩放定律如何非常自然地出现在一个非常

343
00:15:51,880 --> 00:15:55,120
简单的估计问题上。

344
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,420
所以暂时忘掉语言模型。

345
00:15:57,420 --> 00:15:59,140
这是一门语言模型课，

346
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:01,200
但现在它是统计课，接下来的一

347
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,280
张幻灯片，或者实际上是两张幻灯片。

348
00:16:03,280 --> 00:16:06,080
不，也许是三张幻灯片。

349
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:07,720
所以在这种情况下，我想做的是

350
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:10,535
我想从一堆数据中估计均值。

351
00:16:10,535 --> 00:16:12,660
所以我有一个输入，它是从高斯分布中抽取的。

352
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:14,420
我要估计这个对象的均值。

353
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:16,240
我就写下经验均值。

354
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:18,220
然后，你可以写下误差是多少。

355
00:16:18,220 --> 00:16:19,428
那么期望误差是多少？

356
00:16:19,428 --> 00:16:21,415
这些每个都是高斯分布，

357
00:16:21,415 --> 00:16:24,040
而且 mu hat 是高斯分布，所以我可以写下误差。

358
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:26,480
那将是 sigma 平方除以 n。

359
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:30,900
所以，如果你看这个，这就是一个缩放定律。

360
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:35,030
我写下了误差，而误差，如果我取对数，

361
00:16:35,030 --> 00:16:40,590
is a function of log n, log n being the log x-axis.

362
00:16:40,590 --> 00:16:42,710
So in general, if you can come up

363
00:16:42,710 --> 00:16:44,990
with anything that is essentially

364
00:16:44,990 --> 00:16:47,950
1 over n to the alpha plus some constant,

365
00:16:47,950 --> 00:16:50,090
when you plot that on a log-log plot,

366
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:51,510
subtracting out the constant term,

367
00:16:51,510 --> 00:16:53,295
you're going to get a scaling law.

368
00:16:53,295 --> 00:16:55,670
So anything of this form, where you have polynomial decay

369
00:16:55,670 --> 00:16:59,250
and errors, is going to end up giving you a scaling law.

370
00:16:59,250 --> 00:17:02,230
And of course, in parametric estimation like mean estimation

371
00:17:02,230 --> 00:17:05,329
or regression, you of course, expect a polynomial rate.

372
00:17:05,329 --> 00:17:08,829
You don't really expect anything else.

373
00:17:08,829 --> 00:17:11,589
And if you've taken your stats estimation courses,

374
00:17:11,589 --> 00:17:14,030
you know that essentially all the classical

375
00:17:14,030 --> 00:17:17,349
models you are expected to have a 1 over n scaling.

376
00:17:17,349 --> 00:17:19,990
If you fit a regression or so on, you might get D over n

377
00:17:19,990 --> 00:17:21,710
or whatever else.

378
00:17:21,710 --> 00:17:25,470
And so if this is true, and this is the origins of scaling laws,

379
00:17:25,470 --> 00:17:27,710
if we plot our lines, we should expect

380
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:30,830
to see roughly a slope of minus 1

381
00:17:30,830 --> 00:17:32,740
because that's the exponent on the x.

382
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:34,780
Y equals minus x plus C.

383
00:17:34,780 --> 00:17:36,760
If we fit our neural scaling laws,

384
00:17:36,760 --> 00:17:39,740
we will find that our exponents are quite different.

385
00:17:39,740 --> 00:17:43,300
These exponents-- I've taken from these two

386
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:45,780
on the left from Hestenes, I've taken the one on the right

387
00:17:45,780 --> 00:17:46,860
from Kaplan.

388
00:17:46,860 --> 00:17:51,600
They're about -0.1, -0.3, -0.1, roughly speaking.

389
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:53,760
And so what does that mean?

390
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:56,740
That means this is much slower than estimating a mean

391
00:17:56,740 --> 00:17:58,880
or estimating a linear regression.

392
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,540
So this is much, much slower convergence rate, still

393
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:03,740
polynomial, but much slower.

394
00:18:03,740 --> 00:18:05,760
Now this is interesting.

395
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:07,820
It's saying that we're getting these kind

396
00:18:07,820 --> 00:18:11,320
of nice polynomial rates, but the exponents are not so great.

397
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:12,780
They're -0.1.

398
00:18:12,780 --> 00:18:15,460
Where might you get an estimation rate like this?

399
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:19,680
Well, you would get this if your model was more nonparametric.

400
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:21,460
I promise this will be the last statistics

401
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:23,460
slide of this lecture.

402
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:25,100
Let's say that I want to estimate now,

403
00:18:25,100 --> 00:18:28,040
instead of a mean, an arbitrary smooth function.

404
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:31,780
So one thing I could do is, if I'm doing a regression problem,

405
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:34,220
I have my inputs, x1 through xn.

406
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:35,777
Let's say they're in the unit box.

407
00:18:35,777 --> 00:18:37,360
I have a little bit of Gaussian noise,

408
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:40,540
and I have an unknown function f that I would like to estimate.

409
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:44,320
A trivial estimator for this is I can take my space,

410
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:47,100
I can cut it up into little 2D boxes of length n

411
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:49,460
to the negative 1 to the fourth.

412
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:51,160
And then what's our estimation error?

413
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:53,000
I'll have square root n boxes.

414
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,557
Each box is going to get square root n samples.

415
00:18:55,557 --> 00:18:57,140
And the error is going to be roughly 1

416
00:18:57,140 --> 00:18:58,840
over square root of n or something.

417
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:02,400
And in general, if have a D-dimensional function

418
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:06,180
I would like to estimate in this very nonparametric, arbitrary

419
00:19:06,180 --> 00:19:09,340
way, my error is going to be roughly equal to n

420
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:11,040
to the negative 1 over d.

421
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:13,180
So my scaling is going to be y equals negative 1

422
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:14,940
over D times x.

423
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:17,860
And so now these kinds more flexible functions, functions

424
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:20,260
that are more flexible than the linear class,

425
00:19:20,260 --> 00:19:23,940
now have rates in their scaling laws that are not just 1,

426
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:25,080
they're 1 over D.

427
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:28,380
And so one mental model that you could have

428
00:19:28,380 --> 00:19:31,450
is that the neural networks I was showing you in these slides

429
00:19:31,450 --> 00:19:34,610
are behaving kind of non-parametric regressors in 10

430
00:19:34,610 --> 00:19:36,730
dimensions, like a nearest neighbor or other kinds

431
00:19:36,730 --> 00:19:38,750
of estimators in 10 dimensions.

432
00:19:38,750 --> 00:19:41,230
That's roughly the rate at which they learn from data.

433
00:19:41,230 --> 00:19:42,605
That's kind of a cool thing to be

434
00:19:42,605 --> 00:19:46,850
able to know just by looking at some of these scaling laws.

435
00:19:46,850 --> 00:19:49,690
Some people have argued this more strongly.

436
00:19:49,690 --> 00:19:53,010
Bari et al. and some other scaling law theory folks

437
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:56,090
have argued that the scaling law exponents are actually

438
00:19:56,090 --> 00:19:58,530
literally telling us that the neural network

439
00:19:58,530 --> 00:20:00,730
is behaving like a non-parametric smoother,

440
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:02,568
and so on and so forth.

441
00:20:02,568 --> 00:20:04,110
It's an interesting set of arguments,

442
00:20:04,110 --> 00:20:07,330
and you can buy that intuition to whatever extent you'd like.

443
00:20:07,330 --> 00:20:10,490
I don't quite know how much I truly, truly buy this argument.

444
00:20:10,490 --> 00:20:13,350
There's some of the evidence that might be a little sketchy.

445
00:20:13,350 --> 00:20:16,297
It relies on estimators of intrinsic dimension,

446
00:20:16,297 --> 00:20:18,130
but it's an interesting thing to think about

447
00:20:18,130 --> 00:20:20,570
that somehow these exponents are telling us

448
00:20:20,570 --> 00:20:24,490
how fast are these neural networks really learning.

449
00:20:24,490 --> 00:20:27,830
OK, so actually, I'll pause for the moment here.

450
00:20:27,830 --> 00:20:30,360
That's really the end of the really basic data scaling

451
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:33,200
law of one distribution, and you sample from it,

452
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:34,320
and you learn from it.

453
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:37,700
Is that all clear, like the examples and so on?

454
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:38,200
Good.

455
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:39,620
Oh yes, question.

456
00:20:39,620 --> 00:20:42,280
When you say that the model is always larger

457
00:20:42,280 --> 00:20:45,510
than data set size, do you mean one-to-one number of parameters

458
00:20:45,510 --> 00:20:47,652
to number of tokens [INAUDIBLE]?

459
00:20:47,652 --> 00:20:48,860
Yeah, that's a good question.

460
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:49,760
So the question was something like,

461
00:20:49,760 --> 00:20:51,400
What do I mean when I say the model is larger

462
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:52,580
than the data set size?

463
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:58,250
What I mean is when the model gets small relative to the data,

464
00:20:58,250 --> 00:21:00,900
like you have way more data than you have model size,

465
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:03,000
you enter this irreducible error regime.

466
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,400
You basically fitted the data as well

467
00:21:05,400 --> 00:21:07,120
as you can for your model class, and you

468
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:10,280
can't do any better no matter how much data you add.

469
00:21:10,280 --> 00:21:12,740
So we want to--when we're thinking about scaling laws,

470
00:21:12,740 --> 00:21:15,840
we usually want to think about this power law regime, which

471
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:17,060
is the simpler case.

472
00:21:17,060 --> 00:21:19,440
Either that or you explicitly fit the asymptote

473
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:20,618
and then you correct for it.

474
00:21:20,618 --> 00:21:21,660
Those are the two things.

475
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:24,020
So when I say the model is larger than the data,

476
00:21:24,020 --> 00:21:27,095
it can be basically any amount that is larger than the data,

477
00:21:27,095 --> 00:21:28,470
as long as it's in this power law

478
00:21:28,470 --> 00:21:31,710
regime, usually like 10 times bigger than the data, what

479
00:21:31,710 --> 00:21:33,470
have you.

480
00:21:33,470 --> 00:21:34,310
Cool.

481
00:21:34,310 --> 00:21:35,030
Good.

482
00:21:35,030 --> 00:21:39,250
OK, now we are back to other data scaling laws.

483
00:21:39,250 --> 00:21:42,790
So data scaling laws by themselves

484
00:21:42,790 --> 00:21:45,610
are not quite as useful as you'd like them to be.

485
00:21:45,610 --> 00:21:48,450
They just tell you how fast does my model learn.

486
00:21:48,450 --> 00:21:51,250
And that's kind of useful for forecasting.

487
00:21:51,250 --> 00:21:53,870
It's not useful for very much else.

488
00:21:53,870 --> 00:21:55,310
If you want to do engineering, you

489
00:21:55,310 --> 00:21:57,190
are probably interested in other questions,

490
00:21:57,190 --> 00:21:59,730
like what is my optimal data mixture?

491
00:21:59,730 --> 00:22:02,190
What is the best way for me to pick my training

492
00:22:02,190 --> 00:22:03,330
data for my model?

493
00:22:03,330 --> 00:22:05,550
Very useful engineering question.

494
00:22:05,550 --> 00:22:07,790
Should I repeat my data, or should I

495
00:22:07,790 --> 00:22:12,150
just not repeat my data, save the compute for something else?

496
00:22:12,150 --> 00:22:15,350
Or maybe I should repeat some high-quality stuff

497
00:22:15,350 --> 00:22:18,050
and not repeat the low-quality stuff.

498
00:22:18,050 --> 00:22:20,310
I could do various other things with repetition.

499
00:22:20,310 --> 00:22:22,430
There's a lot of different engineering decisions

500
00:22:22,430 --> 00:22:23,450
I can make with data.

501
00:22:23,450 --> 00:22:28,420
So how can scaling laws help us make some of those decisions?

502
00:22:28,420 --> 00:22:30,420
And I think one of the things that's interesting

503
00:22:30,420 --> 00:22:33,820
is if we think a little bit about how classical

504
00:22:33,820 --> 00:22:35,500
models behave.

505
00:22:35,500 --> 00:22:38,340
Data scaling laws, if we think about their origins

506
00:22:38,340 --> 00:22:41,420
as essentially empirical versions of generalization

507
00:22:41,420 --> 00:22:45,700
bounds, they tell us, well, data set composition for many models

508
00:22:45,700 --> 00:22:48,460
really affect the offset of these scaling laws,

509
00:22:48,460 --> 00:22:51,498
but their slopes are actually determined by the model class.

510
00:22:51,498 --> 00:22:54,040
They're not really determined by the distribution themselves.

511
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:55,540
So the slopes might remain the same,

512
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:57,333
and only the intercepts might change.

513
00:22:57,333 --> 00:23:00,000
And the intercept might actually be really interestingly shaped.

514
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,780
So this is a case of a toy model with a linear regression

515
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:05,960
with two different things you can sample from.

516
00:23:05,960 --> 00:23:08,420
If you get only one kind of data,

517
00:23:08,420 --> 00:23:10,280
the errors are high no matter what.

518
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:12,040
You actually want a mixture of both, right.

519
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:15,640
So if you write down how this intercept behaves,

520
00:23:15,640 --> 00:23:18,580
you actually start to get some very interesting insights

521
00:23:18,580 --> 00:23:22,020
about how having more data diversity is very helpful.

522
00:23:22,020 --> 00:23:25,140
But what does this practically mean?

523
00:23:25,140 --> 00:23:27,660
Well, practically speaking, what this means

524
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:31,400
is you can actually fit data scaling laws on mixtures,

525
00:23:31,400 --> 00:23:34,080
and that can help you do some data--

526
00:23:34,080 --> 00:23:37,122
sorry, pre-training, data optimization.

527
00:23:37,122 --> 00:23:38,080
So what does this mean?

528
00:23:38,080 --> 00:23:41,160
Let's say you have two data sources for simplicity.

529
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:43,480
You have news, and you have Wikipedia.

530
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:46,580
And how much news to use and how much Wikipedia to use.

531
00:23:46,580 --> 00:23:48,000
How can you determine that?

532
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,820
Well, you can train really small models

533
00:23:50,820 --> 00:23:52,795
on a really small amount of data.

534
00:23:52,795 --> 00:23:54,420
And then you can basically train or you

535
00:23:54,420 --> 00:23:58,500
can fit a function of how different mixture levels affect

536
00:23:58,500 --> 00:23:59,640
your performance.

537
00:23:59,640 --> 00:24:03,400
You can do this while you scale up your models little by little.

538
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:05,083
And you can see how the trends change.

539
00:24:05,083 --> 00:24:07,500
And you can try to extrapolate that out the exact same way

540
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:09,860
as you would extrapolate out a data scaling law.

541
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:11,900
And then you can ask which one will be optimal

542
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:14,460
if I keep scaling it out to my full training run.

543
00:24:14,460 --> 00:24:18,740
So this is a very simple idea of essentially fitting

544
00:24:18,740 --> 00:24:21,530
a functional form at a small amount of compute,

545
00:24:21,530 --> 00:24:24,610
finding the minimum, and then scaling that guy out.

546
00:24:24,610 --> 00:24:28,530
So this is a data mixing loss paper.

547
00:24:28,530 --> 00:24:30,050
Unfortunately, I think if you talk

548
00:24:30,050 --> 00:24:32,810
to anyone who's done a lot of these data mixture work,

549
00:24:32,810 --> 00:24:35,370
they will tell you reality is a lot more

550
00:24:35,370 --> 00:24:38,730
noisy than this ideal world would suggest.

551
00:24:38,730 --> 00:24:42,450
And really, what has, as far as I know, happened in many cases

552
00:24:42,450 --> 00:24:45,430
is you end up training a bunch of small models.

553
00:24:45,430 --> 00:24:48,010
You pick the best data mix from the small model,

554
00:24:48,010 --> 00:24:51,250
and you just scale that guy up, no scaling law required.

555
00:24:51,250 --> 00:24:53,930
And data decide if you're interested in this kind of thing

556
00:24:53,930 --> 00:24:58,650
is a nice, large-scale empirical study of these kinds of data

557
00:24:58,650 --> 00:24:59,930
mixture things.

558
00:24:59,930 --> 00:25:01,710
And what they found was actually,

559
00:25:01,710 --> 00:25:03,890
if you don't fit a scaling law, and instead you

560
00:25:03,890 --> 00:25:06,350
just pick the best data mix at the small scale,

561
00:25:06,350 --> 00:25:07,810
that works well.

562
00:25:07,810 --> 00:25:10,290
For what it's worth, that's consistent with the argument

563
00:25:10,290 --> 00:25:13,250
that the intercepts differ, but the slopes don't change.

564
00:25:13,250 --> 00:25:15,150
Because if the slopes don't change,

565
00:25:15,150 --> 00:25:17,850
the best mixture at small scale is also the best mixture

566
00:25:17,850 --> 00:25:20,080
at large scale.

567
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:23,760
OK, another really interesting set

568
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:27,800
of questions that I'll also mention in the context of data

569
00:25:27,800 --> 00:25:29,440
is repetition.

570
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:33,820
I think it's increasingly the case that compute is growing,

571
00:25:33,820 --> 00:25:35,932
the amount of data that we have is not growing.

572
00:25:35,932 --> 00:25:37,640
And so there's a lot of people interested

573
00:25:37,640 --> 00:25:40,160
in questions of what happens when you repeat

574
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:42,600
data more and more times.

575
00:25:42,600 --> 00:25:45,720
And there was a nice study a few years back called "Scaling Data-

576
00:25:45,720 --> 00:25:48,000
Constrained Language Models," where they roughly show

577
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,300
something like up to four epochs with standard training recipes,

578
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:53,280
you just don't get hurt at all.

579
00:25:53,280 --> 00:25:56,040
But if you go past that point, then actually

580
00:25:56,040 --> 00:25:58,840
your realized scaling law, this dark curve

581
00:25:58,840 --> 00:26:01,543
is much worse than the projected scaling law

582
00:26:01,543 --> 00:26:02,460
if you had fresh data.

583
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:04,217
That's the dashed line over here.

584
00:26:04,217 --> 00:26:06,800
And you can also, once again, write down a modified functional

585
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:11,120
form that's generally predictive of the behavior of this scaling

586
00:26:11,120 --> 00:26:14,440
law under repetition.

587
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:17,140
And we can take this idea even more to the extreme.

588
00:26:17,140 --> 00:26:19,990
This was kind of recent work with Percy

589
00:26:19,990 --> 00:26:21,530
in one of our co-advise students,

590
00:26:21,530 --> 00:26:23,030
where we were asking, like, OK, what

591
00:26:23,030 --> 00:26:25,030
happens if you take this idea to the extreme

592
00:26:25,030 --> 00:26:27,570
and you just consider infinite amounts of compute?

593
00:26:27,570 --> 00:26:29,850
So you're allowed to epoch as many times as you want,

594
00:26:29,850 --> 00:26:31,332
like an infinite amount of times.

595
00:26:31,332 --> 00:26:33,790
What is the best thing that you can get out of this system?

596
00:26:33,790 --> 00:26:35,350
Well, it turns out you can't just

597
00:26:35,350 --> 00:26:37,422
keep repeating passes over the data.

598
00:26:37,422 --> 00:26:39,130
You can't keep making your models bigger.

599
00:26:39,130 --> 00:26:40,590
Those have diminishing returns.

600
00:26:40,590 --> 00:26:42,570
And so you end up reaching for other things,

601
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:45,342
like ensembling your models to try to squeeze more and more

602
00:26:45,342 --> 00:26:46,050
out of your data.

603
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:48,258
But really, I think the one thing that I'll point out

604
00:26:48,258 --> 00:26:50,350
that's really interesting is your standard data

605
00:26:50,350 --> 00:26:51,690
scaling laws here in the red.

606
00:26:51,690 --> 00:26:55,110
It's a very nice, predictable improvement in performance

607
00:26:55,110 --> 00:26:56,810
as you increase the amount of data.

608
00:26:56,810 --> 00:26:59,830
We do all sorts of interventions like regularizing and adding

609
00:26:59,830 --> 00:27:00,950
ensembles.

610
00:27:00,950 --> 00:27:02,850
You get improvements in performance,

611
00:27:02,850 --> 00:27:05,690
but the slopes look actually surprisingly similar.

612
00:27:05,690 --> 00:27:08,117
This is a lesson that you'll learn

613
00:27:08,117 --> 00:27:10,450
once you start fitting your own scaling laws, that very,

614
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:13,410
very often your slopes don't change.

615
00:27:13,410 --> 00:27:15,150
Often, the interventions that you do just

616
00:27:15,150 --> 00:27:19,710
change the intercept of the scaling laws.

617
00:27:19,710 --> 00:27:22,070
One last thing that I'll mention,

618
00:27:22,070 --> 00:27:24,550
because I like this paper in the context of scaling

619
00:27:24,550 --> 00:27:27,670
laws and scaling in general, is that a lot

620
00:27:27,670 --> 00:27:30,070
of the phenomena we care about in data

621
00:27:30,070 --> 00:27:31,950
is actually very scale-dependent.

622
00:27:31,950 --> 00:27:34,790
And one example of this is data filtering.

623
00:27:34,790 --> 00:27:37,870
So if you or I tomorrow decide to filter some data

624
00:27:37,870 --> 00:27:39,870
for the models we're training, we would probably

625
00:27:39,870 --> 00:27:41,090
filter very aggressively.

626
00:27:41,090 --> 00:27:43,590
We would only keep the highest quality stuff

627
00:27:43,590 --> 00:27:45,770
because both you and I don't have very much compute.

628
00:27:45,770 --> 00:27:48,050
So we can't train on all the internet anyway.

629
00:27:48,050 --> 00:27:52,190
We might as well filter down to the most high-quality stuff.

630
00:27:52,190 --> 00:27:54,635
On the other hand, if you have a lot of compute,

631
00:27:54,635 --> 00:27:56,510
then you want to train on more and more stuff

632
00:27:56,510 --> 00:27:58,470
because you're going to not want to repeat

633
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:00,150
on this very high-quality data.

634
00:28:00,150 --> 00:28:01,690
So as you get more and more compute,

635
00:28:01,690 --> 00:28:03,958
your filter has become looser and looser,

636
00:28:03,958 --> 00:28:05,750
and potentially you start training on stuff

637
00:28:05,750 --> 00:28:07,830
that's lower and lower quality.

638
00:28:07,830 --> 00:28:09,390
So a lot of the things that we often

639
00:28:09,390 --> 00:28:11,710
think about statically, things like data

640
00:28:11,710 --> 00:28:13,578
filtering or the quality of data,

641
00:28:13,578 --> 00:28:14,870
are actually much more dynamic.

642
00:28:14,870 --> 00:28:17,305
As you increase scale, you have to think about

643
00:28:17,305 --> 00:28:19,180
where am I going to get the rest of the data.

644
00:28:19,180 --> 00:28:22,000
The filters can't stay fixed as a function of the data,

645
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:23,580
and certainly the optimal filters

646
00:28:23,580 --> 00:28:25,960
turn out to not be fixed as a function of scale.

647
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:31,838
OK, so that's it for data scaling laws.

648
00:28:31,838 --> 00:28:34,640
This is the part that I think is most straightforward to understand.

649
00:28:34,640 --> 00:28:37,720
Even as a stats/ML-minded person,

650
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:40,880
this stuff is all very simple and straightforward to me.

651
00:28:40,880 --> 00:28:42,820
You increase data, performance gets better

652
00:28:42,820 --> 00:28:44,060
in a very predictable way.

653
00:28:44,060 --> 00:28:45,720
I think this is uncontroversial.

654
00:28:45,720 --> 00:28:47,980
The fact that it does so polynomially for such

655
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:52,220
a big model is still cool, but nothing really crazy.

656
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,020
So hopefully this is reasonable to all of you

657
00:28:55,020 --> 00:28:57,860
that there's so much predictability when you

658
00:28:57,860 --> 00:29:00,900
increase the amount of data.

659
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:02,940
Oh, yes.

660
00:29:02,940 --> 00:29:05,140
So on this slide for the pre-training

661
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:10,620
under infinite compute, so that graph on the right

662
00:29:10,620 --> 00:29:16,450
is linear in both the loss and data set size,

663
00:29:16,450 --> 00:29:20,150
whereas the previous example I gave was log-log scale.

664
00:29:20,150 --> 00:29:22,450
And I was wondering why it's linear in both.

665
00:29:22,450 --> 00:29:25,750
Yeah, so this is a bad axis.

666
00:29:25,750 --> 00:29:28,890
Herman and I just talked about bad axis like this.

667
00:29:28,890 --> 00:29:30,390
This is actually doubling.

668
00:29:30,390 --> 00:29:32,390
So it looks linear, but it's actually log scale.

669
00:29:32,390 --> 00:29:33,730
[LAUGHS] I fixed it now.

670
00:29:33,730 --> 00:29:35,210
You fixed it now.

671
00:29:35,210 --> 00:29:38,310
And the y-axis here, it doesn't look logged,

672
00:29:38,310 --> 00:29:39,723
but the range of this is so small

673
00:29:39,723 --> 00:29:41,390
that linear logs are basically the same.

674
00:29:41,390 --> 00:29:43,510
If you look at the fitted functional forms here,

675
00:29:43,510 --> 00:29:44,750
these are still polynomials.

676
00:29:44,750 --> 00:29:46,542
So we're fitting the same functional forms,

677
00:29:46,542 --> 00:29:48,773
and those are the ones that are plotted here.

678
00:29:48,773 --> 00:29:49,690
This is another point.

679
00:29:49,690 --> 00:29:52,850
I'll get to it at the end of the lecture.

680
00:29:52,850 --> 00:29:55,610
If you only have a tiny slice of a compute range,

681
00:29:55,610 --> 00:29:57,170
很难非常、非常明确地分辨

682
00:29:57,170 --> 00:29:58,770
某个东西是按多项式缩放的

683
00:29:58,770 --> 00:30:00,550
还是按指数缩放的。

684
00:30:00,550 --> 00:30:02,510
因为泰勒近似确实存在。

685
00:30:02,510 --> 00:30:05,070
只要放大得足够多，一切看起来都是线性的。

686
00:30:05,070 --> 00:30:07,753
所以它究竟是多项式

687
00:30:07,753 --> 00:30:09,170
还是某种其他函数形式，你

688
00:30:09,170 --> 00:30:13,500
总是要稍微小心和保持怀疑。

689
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:14,000
好。

690
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,120
OK。

691
00:30:16,120 --> 00:30:19,560
好，所以这就是关于数据的缩放定律，

692
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:21,080
在很多方面都很直接。

693
00:30:21,080 --> 00:30:24,840
现在我想谈谈更奇特的缩放形式

694
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:29,217
以及各种模型工程的缩放定律。

695
00:30:29,217 --> 00:30:30,800
所以我们现在要做的是，

696
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:34,480
尝试设计大型语言模型。

697
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:37,745
也许你是个激进派，相信也许 LSTM

698
00:30:37,745 --> 00:30:38,370
才是未来。

699
00:30:38,370 --> 00:30:41,480
比如，为什么我不能用 LSTM 代替 transformer？

700
00:30:41,480 --> 00:30:44,380
我想把我的 B200 算力花在我的 LSTM 上。

701
00:30:44,380 --> 00:30:46,600
或者也许你是另一种激进派，

702
00:30:46,600 --> 00:30:48,020
想用 SGD 来训练。

703
00:30:48,020 --> 00:30:50,600
比如，为什么我不能用 SGD 训练？

704
00:30:50,600 --> 00:30:51,780
你可能也会想。

705
00:30:51,780 --> 00:30:54,300
如果你的资源有限，

706
00:30:54,300 --> 00:30:56,760
你有不同的方式来分配它。

707
00:30:56,760 --> 00:30:58,417
也许你应该把模型训练更久，

708
00:30:58,417 --> 00:31:00,000
也许你训练更大的模型，也许我

709
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:01,380
应该去收集更多数据。

710
00:31:01,380 --> 00:31:03,640
总是有各种各样的权衡在起作用，

711
00:31:03,640 --> 00:31:06,720
而缩放定律给了我们定量的方式来做

712
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,040
这些权衡，希望如此。

713
00:31:10,040 --> 00:31:12,400
我要从一些简单的超参数

714
00:31:12,400 --> 00:31:15,680
问题开始，比如我如何选择我的架构

715
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:18,000
或优化器，这些我在

716
00:31:18,000 --> 00:31:20,120
第二讲中谈过的东西。

717
00:31:20,120 --> 00:31:22,640
在那里我给了你这样的视角：也许

718
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:24,783
你可以研究别人怎么做。

719
00:31:24,783 --> 00:31:27,200
现在我要试着给你一些第一性原理的答案

720
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:29,640
来回答那些问题，用稍微不同的视角

721
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:30,360
of trade-offs.

722
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,820
So you have to believe in scaling laws in this lecture,

723
00:31:32,820 --> 00:31:36,160
but as long as you do, you get to try things on your own

724
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:37,440
and find out.

725
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:40,840
OK, the very first question we might start with is to say,

726
00:31:40,840 --> 00:31:44,320
Are transformers really better than LSTMs?

727
00:31:44,320 --> 00:31:45,320
LSTMs.

728
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:46,640
They can work OK, too.

729
00:31:46,640 --> 00:31:48,500
They will fit distributions.

730
00:31:48,500 --> 00:31:50,660
With the advent of things like Mamba,

731
00:31:50,660 --> 00:31:53,000
we know that SSMs can work.

732
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:54,768
So are they better?

733
00:31:54,768 --> 00:31:56,560
The brute force way to answer this question

734
00:31:56,560 --> 00:32:02,400
is to train a big LSTM GPT-3 or even bigger class of models.

735
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:06,610
The scaling law way would be to train a bunch of smaller models

736
00:32:06,610 --> 00:32:08,360
and say, I'm going to train my transformer

737
00:32:08,360 --> 00:32:11,008
across a different variety of compute ranges,

738
00:32:11,008 --> 00:32:13,550
and I'm going to train different LSTMs with different numbers

739
00:32:13,550 --> 00:32:15,630
of layers on, once again, a variety

740
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:17,750
of different compute ranges.

741
00:32:17,750 --> 00:32:19,130
Now, what does this show?

742
00:32:19,130 --> 00:32:23,990
This shows LSTMs have definitely different intercept, maybe even

743
00:32:23,990 --> 00:32:25,890
different slope than the transformer.

744
00:32:25,890 --> 00:32:30,390
And because of this, probably you don't want to pick LSTM.

745
00:32:30,390 --> 00:32:33,510
This plot would justify scaling up the transformer instead

746
00:32:33,510 --> 00:32:34,625
of the LSTM.

747
00:32:34,625 --> 00:32:36,750
And if you look at a lot of the architecture papers

748
00:32:36,750 --> 00:32:38,250
today, like you go look at the Mamba

749
00:32:38,250 --> 00:32:40,575
paper or the gated delta net papers,

750
00:32:40,575 --> 00:32:41,950
they will always have a plot that

751
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:45,510
looks like this, where you have vanilla transformer, our really

752
00:32:45,510 --> 00:32:46,250
cool model.

753
00:32:46,250 --> 00:32:48,843
And our really cool models, usually either on top or below,

754
00:32:48,843 --> 00:32:51,510
because that's what would prove that your model is doing better.

755
00:32:51,510 --> 00:32:54,310
And you certainly don't want something where your slopes are

756
00:32:54,310 --> 00:32:56,970
worse because that means as you scale up larger and larger,

757
00:32:56,970 --> 00:32:58,950
your models will eventually do worse.

758
00:32:58,950 --> 00:33:01,310
So this is one way of really reasoning

759
00:33:01,310 --> 00:33:08,020
about architectures without training gigantic models.

760
00:33:08,020 --> 00:33:10,220
There haven't been, I think, quite

761
00:33:10,220 --> 00:33:15,820
as many recent and good architecture scaling law papers.

762
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:18,980
I think the one that is relatively recent

763
00:33:18,980 --> 00:33:22,858
and that I like quite a lot is by Ek and others at Google,

764
00:33:22,858 --> 00:33:24,900
back when they were publishing this kind of work,

765
00:33:24,900 --> 00:33:28,660
where they did a whole bunch of scaling studies on T5-style

766
00:33:28,660 --> 00:33:32,660
models, where they trained larger and larger variants of T5

767
00:33:32,660 --> 00:33:35,692
models on many different architectures.

768
00:33:35,692 --> 00:33:37,400
And I think one thing that's really cool,

769
00:33:37,400 --> 00:33:39,820
and one of the reasons why I still bring this paper up,

770
00:33:39,820 --> 00:33:43,340
is they capture exactly the architecture

771
00:33:43,340 --> 00:33:46,920
changes that we implement today in our frontier models.

772
00:33:46,920 --> 00:33:51,620
So things like performer, which is an efficient attention, that

773
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:52,680
doesn't scale very well.

774
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:55,100
We do not implement those.

775
00:33:55,100 --> 00:33:57,880
The things that we implement are the gated linear unit.

776
00:33:57,880 --> 00:34:01,028
Red line better than green line throughout the scaling trends.

777
00:34:01,028 --> 00:34:01,820
Switch transformer.

778
00:34:01,820 --> 00:34:03,695
I don't know what happened with their biggest run,

779
00:34:03,695 --> 00:34:07,130
but generally speaking, it's got good scaling trends.

780
00:34:07,130 --> 00:34:09,971
And I forgot, oh, mixture of softmaxes.

781
00:34:09,971 --> 00:34:11,429
Actually, we don't do this anymore,

782
00:34:11,429 --> 00:34:13,389
but this is probably an effective intervention

783
00:34:13,389 --> 00:34:15,330
given the scaling trends.

784
00:34:15,330 --> 00:34:18,370
So you see that through these scaling trends,

785
00:34:18,370 --> 00:34:20,889
even though a lot of these papers

786
00:34:20,889 --> 00:34:23,090
were operating on much, much smaller compute scales

787
00:34:23,090 --> 00:34:25,250
than what we operate today, they're

788
00:34:25,250 --> 00:34:27,530
able to see the kinds of architecture trends

789
00:34:27,530 --> 00:34:31,429
that are driving frontier model development back then.

790
00:34:31,429 --> 00:34:35,050
And I think this is why a lot of people use scaling laws

791
00:34:35,050 --> 00:34:38,270
as a really, almost paradigmatic way, saying, "Oh,

792
00:34:38,270 --> 00:34:40,300
if it doesn't show up in the scaling law,

793
00:34:40,300 --> 00:34:41,550
it's not a good intervention."

794
00:34:41,550 --> 00:34:43,969
Because in some ways, a lot of the past work

795
00:34:43,969 --> 00:34:47,370
has captured what we see today.

796
00:34:47,370 --> 00:34:48,690
Good.

797
00:34:48,690 --> 00:34:51,610
OK, going beyond architectures, we

798
00:34:51,610 --> 00:34:54,250
can extend this kind of analysis to all sorts of other things

799
00:34:54,250 --> 00:34:57,390
that we might want to understand.

800
00:34:57,390 --> 00:35:00,613
We might ask, OK, is SGD better or worse than ADAM?

801
00:35:00,613 --> 00:35:02,530
If you do the scaling law analysis, this one's

802
00:35:02,530 --> 00:35:05,372
by Hestness, you'll see very clear trends.

803
00:35:05,372 --> 00:35:07,330
This is once again, the really mysterious thing

804
00:35:07,330 --> 00:35:11,450
of the intercepts are different, but the slopes are very similar.

805
00:35:11,450 --> 00:35:13,490
If you fix the data and you fix the models,

806
00:35:13,490 --> 00:35:15,190
slopes are often very, very similar.

807
00:35:15,190 --> 00:35:17,910
I am very surprised by this every time I see it.

808
00:35:17,910 --> 00:35:19,130
And yet, it's very true.

809
00:35:19,130 --> 00:35:21,590
It's rare to get different slopes,

810
00:35:21,590 --> 00:35:24,010
even with an intervention as big as SGD to ADAM.

811
00:35:24,010 --> 00:35:27,150
If you were the one training with SGD or ADAM,

812
00:35:27,150 --> 00:35:29,130
you would think that's a huge change.

813
00:35:29,130 --> 00:35:31,290
Scaling trends remain roughly the same.

814
00:35:31,290 --> 00:35:33,730
In this case, this is a language modeling

815
00:35:33,730 --> 00:35:35,190
with a recurrent highway net.

816
00:35:38,170 --> 00:35:42,470
Similarly, in lecture 2, I told you about aspect ratios.

817
00:35:42,470 --> 00:35:44,770
How do you pick your depth-to-width trade-off?

818
00:35:44,770 --> 00:35:46,890
And I told you, well, you can just

819
00:35:46,890 --> 00:35:49,990
pick some random number that's somewhat roughly reasonable.

820
00:35:49,990 --> 00:35:52,410
It's like four times the reasonable multiplier.

821
00:35:52,410 --> 00:35:55,310
But if you're doing this kind of scaling analysis,

822
00:35:55,310 --> 00:35:58,030
you could much more precisely what is happening.

823
00:35:58,030 --> 00:36:00,410
So the first thing you could do is

824
00:36:00,410 --> 00:36:02,830
you could look at scaling trends as a function of layers,

825
00:36:02,830 --> 00:36:05,880
and you would immediately see that having extremely few layers

826
00:36:05,880 --> 00:36:07,460
is very, very bad for your model.

827
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:09,860
If you have one layer, you're not going anywhere.

828
00:36:09,860 --> 00:36:11,920
That's a terrible, terrible scaling trend.

829
00:36:11,920 --> 00:36:13,480
Greater than 1 layer, actually, it's

830
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:15,600
surprisingly more competitive, although you

831
00:36:15,600 --> 00:36:18,840
do see that at every compute level, more layers, at least

832
00:36:18,840 --> 00:36:21,468
in this case is better.

833
00:36:21,468 --> 00:36:23,760
And if you can do a much more fine-grained study, which

834
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:28,440
they do in Kaplan, where you try to identify what

835
00:36:28,440 --> 00:36:30,420
you might call scale-invariant quantities,

836
00:36:30,420 --> 00:36:32,000
like the aspect ratio.

837
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,620
The number of layers is not a scale-invariant quantity.

838
00:36:34,620 --> 00:36:38,120
As you make your model bigger, you do want more layers.

839
00:36:38,120 --> 00:36:40,020
But as you make your model bigger,

840
00:36:40,020 --> 00:36:43,360
maybe the aspect ratio, the optimal one should stay the same.

841
00:36:43,360 --> 00:36:45,040
This one is scale invariant.

842
00:36:45,040 --> 00:36:47,020
And so if we look at the aspect ratio,

843
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:49,960
we see that at different model sizes,

844
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:53,880
we get basically the same minima around 100d model for every

845
00:36:53,880 --> 00:36:56,440
layer, or maybe a little bit less.

846
00:36:56,440 --> 00:36:58,180
And it does shift a little bit right.

847
00:36:58,180 --> 00:37:01,950
There is a shift towards aspect ratio

848
00:37:01,950 --> 00:37:04,810
that is smaller for deeper models, but for the most part,

849
00:37:04,810 --> 00:37:07,030
the minima stays roughly similar.

850
00:37:07,030 --> 00:37:09,003
You can do similar kinds of analysis

851
00:37:09,003 --> 00:37:11,170
for things like attention head dimension and others.

852
00:37:11,170 --> 00:37:12,910
And if you look at the Kaplan paper,

853
00:37:12,910 --> 00:37:15,330
they do quite a good job at doing all of this.

854
00:37:15,330 --> 00:37:18,270
And this is one way that you can be confident about your scaling

855
00:37:18,270 --> 00:37:19,030
strategy.

856
00:37:19,030 --> 00:37:21,870
So if your scaling strategy is "I'm going to fix my aspect

857
00:37:21,870 --> 00:37:23,410
ratio, and I'm going to scale up,"

858
00:37:23,410 --> 00:37:26,670
you can make plots like this and convince yourself we're probably

859
00:37:26,670 --> 00:37:29,790
good because our optimization shifting too much as I go

860
00:37:29,790 --> 00:37:31,690
to larger and larger and larger models.

861
00:37:34,910 --> 00:37:39,110
One thing that's important, and this is a huge can of worms,

862
00:37:39,110 --> 00:37:40,750
and a sufficiently big can of worms

863
00:37:40,750 --> 00:37:43,210
that I will talk about it on more than one slide,

864
00:37:43,210 --> 00:37:47,410
I will come back, not all parameters are created equal.

865
00:37:47,410 --> 00:37:49,690
And because not all parameters are created equal,

866
00:37:49,690 --> 00:37:51,910
your scaling laws may look good or bad

867
00:37:51,910 --> 00:37:55,870
depending on how you define what a parameter is.

868
00:37:55,870 --> 00:37:58,790
So in the Kaplan paper, they made this observation that

869
00:37:58,790 --> 00:38:02,900
if they drew their scaling laws for depth with the embedding

870
00:38:02,900 --> 00:38:05,540
parameters, they got these very funky-looking scaling laws,

871
00:38:05,540 --> 00:38:08,245
and they said, "Oh, this is no good at all, no good at all."

872
00:38:08,245 --> 00:38:09,620
Because it's no good at all, they

873
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:11,780
decided that they were going to exclude

874
00:38:11,780 --> 00:38:14,300
all the embedding parameters, and they were only

875
00:38:14,300 --> 00:38:17,520
going to count the non-embedding parameters because you could

876
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:19,020
justify it to yourself, saying these

877
00:38:19,020 --> 00:38:23,363
are the parameters that are doing computation or whatever.

878
00:38:23,363 --> 00:38:27,620
We're going to see later that this going to have non-trivial implications on the validity

879
00:38:27,620 --> 00:38:28,960
of some of the results.

880
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:31,720
But the point here is scaling laws aren't magic.

881
00:38:31,720 --> 00:38:33,820
I think Percy likes to make this point,

882
00:38:33,820 --> 00:38:36,940
that scaling laws and all these kinds of predictability

883
00:38:36,940 --> 00:38:38,700
across scales is engineered.

884
00:38:38,700 --> 00:38:40,480
They don't happen automatically.

885
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:43,677
We need to pick the right kinds of x-axes to look at.

886
00:38:43,677 --> 00:38:46,260
We need to make sure that the hyperparameters for these things

887
00:38:46,260 --> 00:38:48,620
are set right, and only under those conditions

888
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:51,300
does it become possible to get predictable scaling

889
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:53,480
across many orders of magnitude compute.

890
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:57,520
So this is what a lot of scaling law researchers do.

891
00:38:57,520 --> 00:38:59,140
They think about what the right way

892
00:38:59,140 --> 00:39:01,980
is to get very predictable scaling.

893
00:39:01,980 --> 00:39:04,380
Now, related to this, I think are

894
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:07,780
very interesting recent papers on things like scaling

895
00:39:07,780 --> 00:39:09,460
for mixture of experts.

896
00:39:09,460 --> 00:39:11,960
Now, that mixture of experts is, you would say,

897
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:15,380
the dominant way of training large models,

898
00:39:15,380 --> 00:39:18,020
it is quite important to think about what is the value

899
00:39:18,020 --> 00:39:20,180
or what is a parameter in this new world

900
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:22,860
where the number of parameters and the number of parameters

901
00:39:22,860 --> 00:39:24,500
are kind of decoupled.

902
00:39:24,500 --> 00:39:26,780
And there's a nice, clean analysis

903
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:30,460
by some folks at Apple and MIT that

904
00:39:30,460 --> 00:39:36,220
show the scaling trends as a function of both parameters

905
00:39:36,220 --> 00:39:37,640
and active parameters.

906
00:39:37,640 --> 00:39:40,140
And the cool thing that you see is,

907
00:39:40,140 --> 00:39:43,040
as you start to train bigger and bigger models,

908
00:39:43,040 --> 00:39:45,460
if you increase the amount of total parameters

909
00:39:45,460 --> 00:39:49,220
on the x-axis over here, and you want to minimize your loss,

910
00:39:49,220 --> 00:39:52,240
you're going to end up with more sparse and sparse models.

911
00:39:52,240 --> 00:39:55,340
See how the color becomes darker and darker and darker.

912
00:39:55,340 --> 00:39:59,530
Similarly, here, if you like the number of active parameters

913
00:39:59,530 --> 00:40:03,370
decreases as a function of MoE sparsity.

914
00:40:03,370 --> 00:40:05,730
And you get the result that I think

915
00:40:05,730 --> 00:40:07,450
is somewhat intuitive here, which

916
00:40:07,450 --> 00:40:09,450
is you can draw these surfaces.

917
00:40:09,450 --> 00:40:12,890
For every compute amount, you can ask, OK,

918
00:40:12,890 --> 00:40:17,850
would I rather spend that compute on active parameters

919
00:40:17,850 --> 00:40:19,770
and how much sparsity do I want?

920
00:40:19,770 --> 00:40:23,210
I can vary my sparsity without really changing the amount

921
00:40:23,210 --> 00:40:25,290
of compute that I'm spending.

922
00:40:25,290 --> 00:40:27,130
Now, given that you can ask, OK, what

923
00:40:27,130 --> 00:40:29,590
happens if I add more of empty parameters,

924
00:40:29,590 --> 00:40:32,250
same active parameters, but more total parameters?

925
00:40:32,250 --> 00:40:34,570
As you move from back on the plot

926
00:40:34,570 --> 00:40:39,090
to in the front of the plot, you see the losses are improving.

927
00:40:39,090 --> 00:40:41,330
So in other words, this is saying

928
00:40:41,330 --> 00:40:43,810
that the parameters in an MoE that are not active

929
00:40:43,810 --> 00:40:45,843
are still helping you reduce your loss, which

930
00:40:45,843 --> 00:40:46,510
is kind of cool.

931
00:40:46,510 --> 00:40:49,110
And you can write down what the functional forms of this are.

932
00:40:49,110 --> 00:40:51,570
I won't go into that part in more detail,

933
00:40:51,570 --> 00:40:54,890
but the point here is to say all of these kinds of quantities

934
00:40:54,890 --> 00:40:57,440
that we care about in optimizing MoE

935
00:40:57,440 --> 00:41:00,620
are things that have predictable scaling and regularities.

936
00:41:03,700 --> 00:41:07,960
And the two things I want to now talk about are actually really--

937
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:10,400
yeah, two things that I now want to talk about.

938
00:41:10,400 --> 00:41:12,760
When you're going to train a new big model,

939
00:41:12,760 --> 00:41:15,200
actually, most of the things that I talk about are fixed.

940
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:16,920
You're probably all not radical enough

941
00:41:16,920 --> 00:41:18,340
that you're going to switch to an LSTM.

942
00:41:18,340 --> 00:41:20,382
You're probably going to pick like, I don't know,

943
00:41:20,382 --> 00:41:23,960
a DeepSeek-V4-inspired MoE or something.

944
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:26,880
And you're probably going to pick architecture choices that

945
00:41:26,880 --> 00:41:27,980
are tried and tested.

946
00:41:27,980 --> 00:41:30,000
You're not going to really throw everything out.

947
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:31,840
But two things that you really do

948
00:41:31,840 --> 00:41:34,440
have to maybe reinvent in some sense

949
00:41:34,440 --> 00:41:36,600
is the batch size and the learning rate.

950
00:41:36,600 --> 00:41:38,400
If you train your deep learning models,

951
00:41:38,400 --> 00:41:41,160
you know that you have to be very careful

952
00:41:41,160 --> 00:41:43,920
about the combination of your batch size and learning rate.

953
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:45,880
You change one, you have to change the other.

954
00:41:45,880 --> 00:41:48,880
Now, batch size, from the previous lecture,

955
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:51,020
is very tricky for another reason.

956
00:41:51,020 --> 00:41:53,180
We want the batch size to be as big as possible

957
00:41:53,180 --> 00:41:55,350
because a big batch size gives us opportunity

958
00:41:55,350 --> 00:41:56,430
for parallelization.

959
00:41:56,430 --> 00:41:59,910
Remember, data parallel requires large batch size.

960
00:41:59,910 --> 00:42:02,550
So now, having said all that, what we really want to

961
00:42:02,550 --> 00:42:05,790
is how large can we make our batch size

962
00:42:05,790 --> 00:42:07,130
before we start to suffer?

963
00:42:07,130 --> 00:42:10,150
That's the relevant systems question.

964
00:42:10,150 --> 00:42:12,350
And you also want to know how does that change

965
00:42:12,350 --> 00:42:14,030
as a function of model size.

966
00:42:14,030 --> 00:42:19,750
So given that preface, there is this very important idea

967
00:42:19,750 --> 00:42:21,615
that is used in a variety of papers

968
00:42:21,615 --> 00:42:22,990
and talked about very frequently,

969
00:42:22,990 --> 00:42:24,990
called the critical batch size.

970
00:42:24,990 --> 00:42:27,510
The critical batch size is roughly this idea that

971
00:42:27,510 --> 00:42:29,230
up until a certain point, when you

972
00:42:29,230 --> 00:42:31,010
start to get diminishing returns,

973
00:42:31,010 --> 00:42:34,610
there are returns, perfect returns, to increased batch size.

974
00:42:34,610 --> 00:42:38,170
So I might call this the noise-limited regime.

975
00:42:38,170 --> 00:42:40,870
So in the noise-limited regime, every additional element

976
00:42:40,870 --> 00:42:44,670
that you throw in your batch reduces the gradient noise

977
00:42:44,670 --> 00:42:45,770
in your SGD step.

978
00:42:45,770 --> 00:42:48,230
And since you're variance-limited,

979
00:42:48,230 --> 00:42:50,770
that reduction in variance is very, very helpful.

980
00:42:50,770 --> 00:42:52,600
That gives you big returns.

981
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:55,000
So this is the regime in which you have perfect scaling.

982
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,100
You're doing about as well as you can,

983
00:42:57,100 --> 00:43:00,940
given the amount of extra examples that you're processing.

984
00:43:00,940 --> 00:43:02,660
As you get to a certain point where

985
00:43:02,660 --> 00:43:05,660
you've reached the noise scale of your gradients,

986
00:43:05,660 --> 00:43:08,880
you get to a point where you are no longer variance-limited.

987
00:43:08,880 --> 00:43:10,320
You're now bias-limited.

988
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:11,360
What does that mean?

989
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:13,000
Well, remember, in gradient descent,

990
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:15,480
you're looking at the local structure of your objective.

991
00:43:15,480 --> 00:43:19,520
You don't have a global view of where the minimum is.

992
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:22,580
So no matter how low noise I am, I'm

993
00:43:22,580 --> 00:43:25,040
only going to point in this light blue direction over here.

994
00:43:25,040 --> 00:43:26,540
And there's always a disagreement

995
00:43:26,540 --> 00:43:28,380
between my local descent direction

996
00:43:28,380 --> 00:43:30,900
and where the global optimum is.

997
00:43:30,900 --> 00:43:32,780
Because of that, at a certain point,

998
00:43:32,780 --> 00:43:34,300
I'm going to get diminishing returns

999
00:43:34,300 --> 00:43:37,800
because now my limiting factor is no longer variance.

1000
00:43:37,800 --> 00:43:39,400
It is this bias term.

1001
00:43:39,400 --> 00:43:44,100
So now I'm bias-limited past this point over here.

1002
00:43:44,100 --> 00:43:46,940
And so critical batch is kind of a rule of thumb,

1003
00:43:46,940 --> 00:43:51,340
you might call it, or a convenient trade-off point where

1004
00:43:51,340 --> 00:43:53,540
you say this is the point at which we're starting

1005
00:43:53,540 --> 00:43:55,780
to cross over from our perfect scaling

1006
00:43:55,780 --> 00:43:59,140
regime to our ineffective scaling regime.

1007
00:43:59,140 --> 00:44:01,097
And so it gives you a good rule of thumb

1008
00:44:01,097 --> 00:44:03,180
of where to be setting your batch size if you want

1009
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:05,860
your batch size to be as big as possible without suffering

1010
00:44:05,860 --> 00:44:08,480
these huge efficiency losses.

1011
00:44:08,480 --> 00:44:11,340
Hopefully, that idea is very clear.

1012
00:44:11,340 --> 00:44:16,060
Now, critical batch size is kind of a strange object.

1013
00:44:16,060 --> 00:44:20,860
It is derived from a variety of complicated calculations

1014
00:44:20,860 --> 00:44:24,940
about the local quadratic approximations to the objective.

1015
00:44:24,940 --> 00:44:28,020
But I will maybe give you the mechanical steps

1016
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:32,420
of what is actually used to estimate this quantity.

1017
00:44:32,420 --> 00:44:36,180
So what you do is first, you pick a target loss.

1018
00:44:36,180 --> 00:44:38,480
Target loss might be some numbers that you want to hit.

1019
00:44:38,480 --> 00:44:40,580
So your goal is to hit this target loss

1020
00:44:40,580 --> 00:44:41,860
as quickly as possible.

1021
00:44:41,860 --> 00:44:44,020
That's my goal right now.

1022
00:44:44,020 --> 00:44:45,820
What I'm going to do is I'm going to sweep

1023
00:44:45,820 --> 00:44:47,980
over all the different batch sizes or number

1024
00:44:47,980 --> 00:44:49,690
of different batch sizes, and I'm

1025
00:44:49,690 --> 00:44:52,130
going to write down the number of steps that I needed

1026
00:44:52,130 --> 00:44:54,730
to reach my target loss, and the number of examples

1027
00:44:54,730 --> 00:44:57,310
that were needed to get to that target loss.

1028
00:44:57,310 --> 00:45:01,250
Of course, number one and two are related by a batch size factor.

1029
00:45:01,250 --> 00:45:03,290
The number of steps times my batch size

1030
00:45:03,290 --> 00:45:05,810
is equal to the number of examples.

1031
00:45:05,810 --> 00:45:11,250
Now, through some calculations, OpenAI folks, McCandless

1032
00:45:11,250 --> 00:45:15,810
and others, argue that this is the rough functional form

1033
00:45:15,810 --> 00:45:19,010
that we should follow, that the number of steps that you need

1034
00:45:19,010 --> 00:45:20,730
and the number of examples that you need

1035
00:45:20,730 --> 00:45:24,090
are inversely related, with this estimate, which

1036
00:45:24,090 --> 00:45:26,170
is the minimum number of possible steps

1037
00:45:26,170 --> 00:45:28,890
and the minimum number of examples,

1038
00:45:28,890 --> 00:45:31,990
normalizing these two quantities.

1039
00:45:31,990 --> 00:45:34,052
And so there's a trade-off between these two.

1040
00:45:34,052 --> 00:45:36,010
If you want to minimize the number of examples,

1041
00:45:36,010 --> 00:45:37,550
you're going to have a lot of steps.

1042
00:45:37,550 --> 00:45:39,170
And if you want to minimize the number of steps,

1043
00:45:39,170 --> 00:45:40,795
you're going to need a lot of examples.

1044
00:45:40,795 --> 00:45:42,190
That's a very natural trade-off.

1045
00:45:42,190 --> 00:45:44,690
And if you increase or decrease your batch size,

1046
00:45:44,690 --> 00:45:48,000
you're going to have trade-offs that go one side or the other.

1047
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:49,960
Now, they argue the goal now should

1048
00:45:49,960 --> 00:45:51,580
be to balance these two sides.

1049
00:45:51,580 --> 00:45:54,080
I want to make sure that my number of steps term

1050
00:45:54,080 --> 00:45:56,560
and the number of examples terms are roughly

1051
00:45:56,560 --> 00:45:58,020
balanced with each other.

1052
00:45:58,020 --> 00:45:59,480
And if you solve for that, you're

1053
00:45:59,480 --> 00:46:01,640
going to get this particular solution, which

1054
00:46:01,640 --> 00:46:04,480
is that the critical batch size is

1055
00:46:04,480 --> 00:46:06,640
the minimum number of examples that I

1056
00:46:06,640 --> 00:46:09,940
need divided by the minimum number of steps that I need.

1057
00:46:09,940 --> 00:46:14,120
And these two are estimated by fitting this to the observed

1058
00:46:14,120 --> 00:46:15,653
data that I have.

1059
00:46:15,653 --> 00:46:17,820
This is going to balance both sides of the equation.

1060
00:46:17,820 --> 00:46:20,920
It's going to give me a little bit more number of steps

1061
00:46:20,920 --> 00:46:21,620
than optimal.

1062
00:46:21,620 --> 00:46:24,700
It's going to require a little bit more examples than optimal,

1063
00:46:24,700 --> 00:46:26,100
but it balances the two sides.

1064
00:46:26,100 --> 00:46:32,640
And you can think of this as optimizing the convergence rate

1065
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:37,000
without blowing up the number of steps that you need.

1066
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:39,375
There's another added point that I won't really

1067
00:46:39,375 --> 00:46:41,500
belabor because this gets even more into the weeds.

1068
00:46:41,500 --> 00:46:43,680
You should really read the critical batch size paper

1069
00:46:43,680 --> 00:46:46,390
if you're interested into this, where one of their arguments

1070
00:46:46,390 --> 00:46:48,690
is estimating this is quite complicated.

1071
00:46:48,690 --> 00:46:51,430
You can do this by just looking at the ratio

1072
00:46:51,430 --> 00:46:53,470
of the gradient covariance in the squared norm

1073
00:46:53,470 --> 00:46:56,390
of your gradient.

1074
00:46:56,390 --> 00:46:59,370
So why is that even in the scaling lecture?

1075
00:46:59,370 --> 00:47:04,550
This is a thing that is not really related to scaling so far.

1076
00:47:04,550 --> 00:47:07,270
The reason why this is in the scaling lecture

1077
00:47:07,270 --> 00:47:09,590
is because, first of all, the critical batch size

1078
00:47:09,590 --> 00:47:11,370
is a reasonable batch size to pick.

1079
00:47:11,370 --> 00:47:13,550
That's a batch size that balances convergence rate

1080
00:47:13,550 --> 00:47:15,110
and the size of the batch.

1081
00:47:15,110 --> 00:47:19,070
So if I want to pick my batch size equal to my critical batch

1082
00:47:19,070 --> 00:47:21,310
size, you might ask, how does this thing

1083
00:47:21,310 --> 00:47:24,370
scale as a function of my target loss?

1084
00:47:24,370 --> 00:47:27,350
Target loss can be thought of as compute.

1085
00:47:27,350 --> 00:47:30,750
If I want to have a model that is better and better and better,

1086
00:47:30,750 --> 00:47:33,690
what is the batch size that I should use?

1087
00:47:33,690 --> 00:47:36,990
And the reason why this isn't the scaling law lecture

1088
00:47:36,990 --> 00:47:40,910
is as you go down in scale in the loss, so your loss is

1089
00:47:40,910 --> 00:47:44,430
improving, the amount of critical batch size increases.

1090
00:47:44,430 --> 00:47:46,750
And it increases in this very predictable way, which

1091
00:47:46,750 --> 00:47:48,590
is once again, a power law.

1092
00:47:48,590 --> 00:47:51,230
So this is the relationship that they argue,

1093
00:47:51,230 --> 00:47:56,430
which is roughly inverse polynomial in batch size.

1094
00:47:56,430 --> 00:47:59,080
So given this, this is actually quite nice

1095
00:47:59,080 --> 00:48:00,830
because what this is saying is if you have

1096
00:48:00,830 --> 00:48:02,910
a really large-scale training run where you go all

1097
00:48:02,910 --> 00:48:04,327
the way to the right of this plot,

1098
00:48:04,327 --> 00:48:06,590
your batch sizes can be quite large.

1099
00:48:06,590 --> 00:48:07,670
And that makes sense.

1100
00:48:07,670 --> 00:48:10,810
As you get closer and closer and closer to the minimum,

1101
00:48:10,810 --> 00:48:13,028
the value of variance reduction increases.

1102
00:48:13,028 --> 00:48:17,030
Because you're trying to optimize finer and finer grained objects, the noise

1103
00:48:17,030 --> 00:48:19,870
matters a lot more once you're minimizing really, really

1104
00:48:19,870 --> 00:48:22,590
tiny differences.

1105
00:48:22,590 --> 00:48:25,990
So that's the batch size story.

1106
00:48:25,990 --> 00:48:27,950
The other side-- so the two quantities,

1107
00:48:27,950 --> 00:48:30,150
remember, that you're going to always have to tune

1108
00:48:30,150 --> 00:48:32,830
is batch size and learning rate.

1109
00:48:32,830 --> 00:48:34,870
The learning rate side of the story

1110
00:48:34,870 --> 00:48:36,530
is also a little bit complicated.

1111
00:48:36,530 --> 00:48:40,310
I'll go into this much more detail in the advanced scaling

1112
00:48:40,310 --> 00:48:42,390
lecture.

1113
00:48:42,390 --> 00:48:44,820
But learning rates generally shift.

1114
00:48:44,820 --> 00:48:46,940
I think this is a good mental picture for how

1115
00:48:46,940 --> 00:48:49,460
to think about learning rates in the standard world

1116
00:48:49,460 --> 00:48:51,540
that you start with, which is, let's say,

1117
00:48:51,540 --> 00:48:53,300
that you have just like a MLP.

1118
00:48:53,300 --> 00:48:55,860
Just a standard neural network of some kind.

1119
00:48:55,860 --> 00:48:57,360
I'm not going to do depth scaling.

1120
00:48:57,360 --> 00:48:59,020
I'm only going to do width scaling.

1121
00:48:59,020 --> 00:49:01,140
Now, if I'm doing width scaling, the bigger

1122
00:49:01,140 --> 00:49:03,820
my model, the bigger my model, the smaller

1123
00:49:03,820 --> 00:49:05,240
my learning rate should be.

1124
00:49:05,240 --> 00:49:07,580
And the reason why my learning rate should be smaller

1125
00:49:07,580 --> 00:49:10,000
is because I have bigger, more parameters,

1126
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:11,480
I'm changing more things at once.

1127
00:49:11,480 --> 00:49:13,460
Maybe I should move less.

1128
00:49:13,460 --> 00:49:15,580
It kind of makes sense.

1129
00:49:15,580 --> 00:49:18,660
And in fact, there's a rule of thumb

1130
00:49:18,660 --> 00:49:21,160
that maybe you want to scale by 1 over the width.

1131
00:49:21,160 --> 00:49:23,460
That's a very commonly known rule of thumb

1132
00:49:23,460 --> 00:49:26,900
that you want to just decrease the learning rate regularly

1133
00:49:26,900 --> 00:49:28,540
as a function of your width, and that

1134
00:49:28,540 --> 00:49:32,340
will give you a rule of thumb to scale your learning rate.

1135
00:49:32,340 --> 00:49:35,620
There is another school of thought,

1136
00:49:35,620 --> 00:49:38,100
or another set of tricks that people do,

1137
00:49:38,100 --> 00:49:40,260
which is that people also rescale

1138
00:49:40,260 --> 00:49:41,710
the network in various ways.

1139
00:49:41,710 --> 00:49:43,810
They will change the initialization sizes,

1140
00:49:43,810 --> 00:49:46,970
and they will change the step sizes

1141
00:49:46,970 --> 00:49:50,650
of the optimizer for various parts of the network

1142
00:49:50,650 --> 00:49:55,250
in order to force the model to have the same learning rate

1143
00:49:55,250 --> 00:49:57,410
minimum across scales.

1144
00:49:57,410 --> 00:50:03,105
And this is an idea that is called muP and others like it.

1145
00:50:03,105 --> 00:50:04,730
Some people have reported great success

1146
00:50:04,730 --> 00:50:06,850
with these kinds of approaches, others

1147
00:50:06,850 --> 00:50:08,450
have reported less success.

1148
00:50:08,450 --> 00:50:11,650
The two ways, basically, of setting a learning rate

1149
00:50:11,650 --> 00:50:14,750
for large-scale runs really is captured in this plot.

1150
00:50:14,750 --> 00:50:16,370
In one way, you can try to estimate

1151
00:50:16,370 --> 00:50:18,290
what these minima are and try to predict

1152
00:50:18,290 --> 00:50:19,890
what your minimum will be.

1153
00:50:19,890 --> 00:50:22,590
And the way in which the minimum changes is pretty predictable,

1154
00:50:22,590 --> 00:50:24,330
so this is not a crazy idea.

1155
00:50:24,330 --> 00:50:26,570
Or you can reparameterize your model

1156
00:50:26,570 --> 00:50:28,910
to try to keep the learning rate minimum the same,

1157
00:50:28,910 --> 00:50:31,390
and then just pick your best learning rate and go.

1158
00:50:31,390 --> 00:50:34,670
Those are the two philosophies to picking your learning rate.

1159
00:50:34,670 --> 00:50:37,630
Both of them have been applied successfully at scale.

1160
00:50:37,630 --> 00:50:39,170
There are large-scale training runs

1161
00:50:39,170 --> 00:50:41,560
that have used both approaches.

1162
00:50:41,560 --> 00:50:46,520
I think anecdotally it does seem more people are maybe favoring

1163
00:50:46,520 --> 00:50:49,560
the scaling law approach, but both are certainly

1164
00:50:49,560 --> 00:50:50,800
viable approaches.

1165
00:50:50,800 --> 00:50:55,600
I'll talk in detail about both of these next lecture.

1166
00:50:55,600 --> 00:51:01,640
So before I get into the last part of this lecture,

1167
00:51:01,640 --> 00:51:03,640
where I want to talk about not quite

1168
00:51:03,640 --> 00:51:09,320
pitfalls but various details of scaling law things,

1169
00:51:09,320 --> 00:51:11,160
one thing I want to end this section with

1170
00:51:11,160 --> 00:51:14,240
is the idea that upstream and downstream performance

1171
00:51:14,240 --> 00:51:15,960
can be quite different.

1172
00:51:15,960 --> 00:51:19,680
So if you look at something like log likelihood,

1173
00:51:19,680 --> 00:51:23,360
this is from the Yi Tay architectures paper,

1174
00:51:23,360 --> 00:51:28,080
you find that perplexity and negative perplexity in this case

1175
00:51:28,080 --> 00:51:29,900
parameters are very correlated.

1176
00:51:29,900 --> 00:51:32,420
The more parameters you have, the better your model,

1177
00:51:32,420 --> 00:51:33,260
very predictably.

1178
00:51:33,260 --> 00:51:35,640
This is a very beautiful linear trend.

1179
00:51:35,640 --> 00:51:37,375
Now you're like, OK, this is great.

1180
00:51:37,375 --> 00:51:39,000
Now we're going to ship our best model.

1181
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:40,360
NL12.

1182
00:51:40,360 --> 00:51:42,420
Turns out NL12 is not your best model.

1183
00:51:42,420 --> 00:51:45,600
It was actually NL32XL, which was a much worse model

1184
00:51:45,600 --> 00:51:46,640
in perplexity.

1185
00:51:46,640 --> 00:51:48,800
This is a very common thing that does happen.

1186
00:51:48,800 --> 00:51:51,603
Upstream and downstream are often correlated.

1187
00:51:51,603 --> 00:51:53,520
This is probably one of the worst correlations

1188
00:51:53,520 --> 00:51:55,378
that I've seen from upstream to downstream.

1189
00:51:55,378 --> 00:51:56,920
But I think it's very, very important

1190
00:51:56,920 --> 00:52:00,440
to remember that scaling laws, generally speaking, are objects

1191
00:52:00,440 --> 00:52:02,500
that you want to apply on the perplexity side.

1192
00:52:02,500 --> 00:52:03,860
They're very clean, they're very regular,

1193
00:52:03,860 --> 00:52:05,040
they're very predictable.

1194
00:52:05,040 --> 00:52:07,400
But transfer from perplexity to downstream

1195
00:52:07,400 --> 00:52:09,880
is a lot less certain than it might initially seem.

1196
00:52:09,880 --> 00:52:13,080
This is a place where you want to be very cautious.

1197
00:52:13,080 --> 00:52:17,040
And as an anecdote, I have former students

1198
00:52:17,040 --> 00:52:19,580
that have gone and done post-training of various places,

1199
00:52:19,580 --> 00:52:20,380
and they always complain.

1200
00:52:20,380 --> 00:52:22,120
They're like, "Oh, those pre-training people,

1201
00:52:22,120 --> 00:52:23,120
they hand you this model.

1202
00:52:23,120 --> 00:52:24,260
They are like, the perplexity is good.

1203
00:52:24,260 --> 00:52:25,580
It's all your problem now."

1204
00:52:25,580 --> 00:52:27,760
But it's often the problems have started

1205
00:52:27,760 --> 00:52:29,140
at the pre-training side.

1206
00:52:29,140 --> 00:52:31,060
So you don't want to be one of those people.

1207
00:52:31,060 --> 00:52:33,060
You want to really think about transfer as well,

1208
00:52:33,060 --> 00:52:36,990
not just focus on perplexities.

1209
00:52:36,990 --> 00:52:39,970
OK, so maybe this is surprising, maybe this is not.

1210
00:52:39,970 --> 00:52:42,230
But the point that I want to drill in here

1211
00:52:42,230 --> 00:52:45,130
is that before you do your big training run,

1212
00:52:45,130 --> 00:52:47,510
you should have a rough idea of what you're going to see.

1213
00:52:47,510 --> 00:52:50,350
Not just is it going to train at all.

1214
00:52:50,350 --> 00:52:51,870
In fact, if you do scaling laws, you

1215
00:52:51,870 --> 00:52:55,310
should be able to actually predict fairly precisely

1216
00:52:55,310 --> 00:52:58,070
the numerical values, almost, of how good

1217
00:52:58,070 --> 00:52:59,110
your run is going to be.

1218
00:52:59,110 --> 00:53:01,250
If I choose one optimizer versus the other,

1219
00:53:01,250 --> 00:53:02,370
what will the gain be?

1220
00:53:02,370 --> 00:53:04,990
You should be able to predict that.

1221
00:53:04,990 --> 00:53:06,670
So the scaling law-based procedure

1222
00:53:06,670 --> 00:53:09,250
is to say I'm going to train a bunch of smaller models,

1223
00:53:09,250 --> 00:53:11,670
I'm going to establish some of a scaling law

1224
00:53:11,670 --> 00:53:14,210
by fitting these lines onto log-log plots.

1225
00:53:14,210 --> 00:53:15,890
And then if the fit is good enough,

1226
00:53:15,890 --> 00:53:18,015
I'm going to have some confidence that the gap will

1227
00:53:18,015 --> 00:53:20,310
persist, and then I'm just going to deploy that

1228
00:53:20,310 --> 00:53:21,910
onto my large-scale training run.

1229
00:53:21,910 --> 00:53:24,970
This is the naive version of a scaling law procedure,

1230
00:53:24,970 --> 00:53:26,790
but I think one that is reasonably

1231
00:53:26,790 --> 00:53:29,970
accurate for how you might think about these things in practice.

1232
00:53:29,970 --> 00:53:31,390
What else are you going to do?

1233
00:53:31,390 --> 00:53:33,610
Just deploy a run out of nowhere?

1234
00:53:33,610 --> 00:53:35,860
Probably not.

1235
00:53:35,860 --> 00:53:37,940
OK, I can pause here and take some questions

1236
00:53:37,940 --> 00:53:42,140
before I get into the last part of this lecture.

1237
00:53:42,140 --> 00:53:43,380
Yes.

1238
00:53:43,380 --> 00:53:45,520
For each of the data points on the scaling law,

1239
00:53:45,520 --> 00:53:47,320
how do you think about variance?

1240
00:53:47,320 --> 00:53:49,320
How many runs do you do?

1241
00:53:49,320 --> 00:53:51,940
And is aggregation usually like the average,

1242
00:53:51,940 --> 00:53:54,660
or do you take min or max depending on [INAUDIBLE]?

1243
00:53:54,660 --> 00:53:59,820
Yeah, I think in most cases, if not all cases that I've seen,

1244
00:53:59,820 --> 00:54:01,380
those are singletons.

1245
00:54:01,380 --> 00:54:03,420
And the reason why it's a singleton

1246
00:54:03,420 --> 00:54:06,060
is because when you're looking at perplexities,

1247
00:54:06,060 --> 00:54:08,480
perplexities are very, very clean.

1248
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:12,282
These objects, if you rerun them,

1249
00:54:12,282 --> 00:54:13,740
they're probably like the variances

1250
00:54:13,740 --> 00:54:16,340
in the second decimal place or something

1251
00:54:16,340 --> 00:54:18,340
because the training data is very

1252
00:54:18,340 --> 00:54:21,740
homogeneous, you've got a lot of it, your eval sets are very big.

1253
00:54:21,740 --> 00:54:23,740
You've really pushed the variance very, very low

1254
00:54:23,740 --> 00:54:25,500
on these things.

1255
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:30,020
But if you're doing something like learning rate or critical batch

1256
00:54:30,020 --> 00:54:34,010
size scaling laws, you will see some truly horrendous stuff.

1257
00:54:34,010 --> 00:54:36,350
I do think people do some variance reduction,

1258
00:54:36,350 --> 00:54:38,770
but it's actually not that common as common

1259
00:54:38,770 --> 00:54:41,970
as it should be, maybe.

1260
00:54:41,970 --> 00:54:43,702
Yes.

1261
00:54:43,702 --> 00:54:47,930
Do people use just downstream metrics directly

1262
00:54:47,930 --> 00:54:49,570
instead of using perplexity?

1263
00:54:49,570 --> 00:54:52,830
Yeah, people do scaling laws directly on downstream metrics,

1264
00:54:52,830 --> 00:54:54,770
but that has the same problem as this.

1265
00:54:54,770 --> 00:54:58,290
So I think the way to think about scaling laws

1266
00:54:58,290 --> 00:55:01,420
is to really reliably establish at least one thing.

1267
00:55:01,420 --> 00:55:03,170
So the way to think about it is, let's say

1268
00:55:03,170 --> 00:55:06,970
I see something like this, I have

1269
00:55:06,970 --> 00:55:08,627
a strong belief that this transformer

1270
00:55:08,627 --> 00:55:09,710
line is going to continue.

1271
00:55:09,710 --> 00:55:11,745
This is a very regular trend.

1272
00:55:11,745 --> 00:55:13,370
And it might curve a little bit, but we

1273
00:55:13,370 --> 00:55:15,328
do think there's probably some regularity here.

1274
00:55:15,328 --> 00:55:18,490
But instead, imagine this was a very noisy jaggedy line

1275
00:55:18,490 --> 00:55:19,510
of this form.

1276
00:55:19,510 --> 00:55:22,070
You would not really know what is going on.

1277
00:55:22,070 --> 00:55:24,190
You would say, I maybe believe that it will go on,

1278
00:55:24,190 --> 00:55:26,450
but maybe it's a line, maybe it's a curve.

1279
00:55:26,450 --> 00:55:28,090
So I think the general--

1280
00:55:28,090 --> 00:55:30,630
if I'm trying to explain the philosophy of this stuff,

1281
00:55:30,630 --> 00:55:33,370
is to say, OK, let's start with the low variance measurement

1282
00:55:33,370 --> 00:55:37,010
and then establish scaling regularity there and then rely

1283
00:55:37,010 --> 00:55:40,690
on some belief about transfer or establish transfer

1284
00:55:40,690 --> 00:55:43,570
or elsewhere to try to show that would transfer over

1285
00:55:43,570 --> 00:55:44,470
to downstream.

1286
00:55:48,530 --> 00:55:50,090
Yeah.

1287
00:55:50,090 --> 00:55:52,490
So some of these are [INAUDIBLE],

1288
00:55:52,490 --> 00:55:54,270
and some are just [INAUDIBLE] loss.

1289
00:55:54,270 --> 00:55:55,770
When do you do either?

1290
00:55:55,770 --> 00:55:57,330
I see, yes.

1291
00:55:57,330 --> 00:56:00,570
You fit to test loss when you're doing good scientific practice,

1292
00:56:00,570 --> 00:56:01,910
like Kaplan 2021.

1293
00:56:01,910 --> 00:56:07,450
But realistically, all the models, except for a very few,

1294
00:56:07,450 --> 00:56:11,430
like the infinite compute one and the repetition stuff,

1295
00:56:11,430 --> 00:56:15,730
unless you're doing some of that, you're doing one-pass SGD.

1296
00:56:15,730 --> 00:56:17,850
So the generalization gap for all of these models

1297
00:56:17,850 --> 00:56:19,130
is very, very small.

1298
00:56:19,130 --> 00:56:21,370
So train and val are very similar.

1299
00:56:21,370 --> 00:56:23,730
I've seen some pre-training code bases that don't even

1300
00:56:23,730 --> 00:56:24,990
have validation loss.

1301
00:56:24,990 --> 00:56:27,570
Because if you're doing one pass, most likely

1302
00:56:27,570 --> 00:56:29,610
your train and val are very, very close.

1303
00:56:29,610 --> 00:56:32,060
That's why a lot of these plots exchange those two,

1304
00:56:32,060 --> 00:56:35,343
because they're all in the one-pass regime.

1305
00:56:35,343 --> 00:56:36,260
Good question, though.

1306
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:40,920
OK, all right, we will continue to move on to--

1307
00:56:40,920 --> 00:56:44,100
sorry, I moved on too far backwards here.

1308
00:56:44,100 --> 00:56:47,680
OK, so now I'm going to talk about one

1309
00:56:47,680 --> 00:56:51,800
of the most well-known uses of a scaling law.

1310
00:56:51,800 --> 00:56:55,340
Even though you might not personally be using this,

1311
00:56:55,340 --> 00:56:58,240
you've certainly used the rule of thumb at some point.

1312
00:56:58,240 --> 00:57:01,080
And also, I think because this set of things

1313
00:57:01,080 --> 00:57:04,400
will tell us something really interesting about the actual

1314
00:57:04,400 --> 00:57:06,680
execution of a scaling law.

1315
00:57:06,680 --> 00:57:09,280
OK, the motivating question is, do I

1316
00:57:09,280 --> 00:57:11,020
want more data or bigger models?

1317
00:57:11,020 --> 00:57:13,640
So the resource that you have, the resource

1318
00:57:13,640 --> 00:57:15,160
that you have to spend, is compute.

1319
00:57:15,160 --> 00:57:17,920
Someone's going to hand you metaphorically a certain amount

1320
00:57:17,920 --> 00:57:18,620
of FLOPs.

1321
00:57:18,620 --> 00:57:21,180
And you're going to take those FLOPs,

1322
00:57:21,180 --> 00:57:22,880
and you're going to spend them somehow

1323
00:57:22,880 --> 00:57:26,320
on—if you remember Percy's very first lecture, it's

1324
00:57:26,320 --> 00:57:28,870
going to be data times the parameters is roughly

1325
00:57:28,870 --> 00:57:30,670
your FLOPs, linear in that.

1326
00:57:30,670 --> 00:57:32,890
So do I want more data or more models?

1327
00:57:32,890 --> 00:57:36,990
Well, we know that if we take a teeny tiny model

1328
00:57:36,990 --> 00:57:40,170
and we dump tons of data into it, that will just be wasted.

1329
00:57:40,170 --> 00:57:43,850
That's the dark purple line at the very top here.

1330
00:57:43,850 --> 00:57:45,830
I've taken a teeny tiny language model,

1331
00:57:45,830 --> 00:57:49,010
and I've passed a huge amount of data into it,

1332
00:57:49,010 --> 00:57:51,330
and this thing has been flat for a very long time.

1333
00:57:51,330 --> 00:57:53,270
This is a complete waste of compute.

1334
00:57:53,270 --> 00:57:55,790
You would have much rather trained this big yellow model

1335
00:57:55,790 --> 00:57:58,030
to the same amount of tokens or even earlier

1336
00:57:58,030 --> 00:58:01,150
on, if you're having the same amount of compute.

1337
00:58:01,150 --> 00:58:06,030
Now, if you could understand the interaction of how

1338
00:58:06,030 --> 00:58:11,270
data amount and model size come together to give you

1339
00:58:11,270 --> 00:58:13,610
performance, if you understood that relationship,

1340
00:58:13,610 --> 00:58:16,010
you could optimize this very precisely.

1341
00:58:16,010 --> 00:58:21,350
And so there are scaling laws that are more advanced

1342
00:58:21,350 --> 00:58:24,890
and relate kind of joint behaviors of objects.

1343
00:58:24,890 --> 00:58:30,100
So almost simultaneously, Kaplan and Rosenfeld

1344
00:58:30,100 --> 00:58:32,920
both proposed these functional forms,

1345
00:58:32,920 --> 00:58:35,740
which are roughly equivalent, describing

1346
00:58:35,740 --> 00:58:38,420
how the size of the model and the amount of data

1347
00:58:38,420 --> 00:58:40,500
relate to the error.

1348
00:58:40,500 --> 00:58:41,840
Rosenfeld is very simple.

1349
00:58:41,840 --> 00:58:44,120
It's really just the sum of two inverse terms.

1350
00:58:44,120 --> 00:58:46,980
These are two scaling laws that are added together.

1351
00:58:46,980 --> 00:58:49,480
And Kaplan is a little bit more complicated,

1352
00:58:49,480 --> 00:58:52,128
but it's roughly the same idea.

1353
00:58:52,128 --> 00:58:53,920
And if you look at, think about the limits,

1354
00:58:53,920 --> 00:58:54,960
this is very intuitive.

1355
00:58:54,960 --> 00:58:58,660
If I have an infinite amount of data, then what happens?

1356
00:58:58,660 --> 00:59:00,260
Then this goes to 0.

1357
00:59:00,260 --> 00:59:01,920
You're basically model-size limited.

1358
00:59:01,920 --> 00:59:03,880
You become a pure model size scaling law.

1359
00:59:03,880 --> 00:59:06,120
If I send my model size to infinity,

1360
00:59:06,120 --> 00:59:08,200
then I'm going to be bounded by data.

1361
00:59:08,200 --> 00:59:10,000
I'm going to have a pure data scaling law.

1362
00:59:10,000 --> 00:59:12,600
So it makes sense if you take the limits of both of these.

1363
00:59:12,600 --> 00:59:15,940
It's often a good idea if someone tells you a scaling law

1364
00:59:15,940 --> 00:59:17,740
to take the limits of all the variables

1365
00:59:17,740 --> 00:59:21,100
to understand the behavior of the system.

1366
00:59:21,100 --> 00:59:22,820
Now, the thing that's kind of cool

1367
00:59:22,820 --> 00:59:25,730
is Rosenfeld and many others that

1368
00:59:25,730 --> 00:59:28,890
have proposed these kinds of joint scaling laws

1369
00:59:28,890 --> 00:59:31,870
show that these things fit quite well, even if you extrapolate.

1370
00:59:31,870 --> 00:59:33,370
So what you're going to do is you're

1371
00:59:33,370 --> 00:59:36,010
going to fit on these green points, which is a low compute

1372
00:59:36,010 --> 00:59:37,910
regime and low data regime.

1373
00:59:37,910 --> 00:59:41,730
You never see things that have either high data or high model

1374
00:59:41,730 --> 00:59:42,430
sizes.

1375
00:59:42,430 --> 00:59:44,570
And then you can extrapolate on the basis

1376
00:59:44,570 --> 00:59:47,250
of these tiny runs in this corner over here

1377
00:59:47,250 --> 00:59:51,850
to essentially this high compute or high data, high model size

1378
00:59:51,850 --> 00:59:52,350
regime.

1379
00:59:52,350 --> 00:59:54,610
And you can very accurately predict the error

1380
00:59:54,610 --> 00:59:56,210
based on your predictions.

1381
00:59:56,210 --> 00:59:58,690
Great predictions.

1382
00:59:58,690 --> 01:00:01,650
And so once you have that, now what you can do is to say,

1383
01:00:01,650 --> 01:00:05,050
well, I have a fixed amount of FLOPs subject to my FLOPs

1384
01:00:05,050 --> 01:00:07,110
constraints, minimize this.

1385
01:00:07,110 --> 01:00:09,830
That's a simple nonlinear optimization problem.

1386
01:00:09,830 --> 01:00:11,850
You can do that to solve for this.

1387
01:00:11,850 --> 01:00:19,250
Now, both Kaplan and Rosenfeld provide equations of this form.

1388
01:00:19,250 --> 01:00:21,790
What do they say?

1389
01:00:21,790 --> 01:00:25,462
Well, if you look at Kaplan, and they solve for this equation,

1390
01:00:25,462 --> 01:00:26,670
they say something like this.

1391
01:00:26,670 --> 01:00:30,710
Well, the amount of--they always flip this.

1392
01:00:30,710 --> 01:00:31,570
It confuses me.

1393
01:00:31,570 --> 01:00:35,950
The amount of data it should be C to the 0.73,

1394
01:00:35,950 --> 01:00:39,722
and the amount of model size should be C to the 0.27.

1395
01:00:39,722 --> 01:00:40,430
Is that reversed?

1396
01:00:40,430 --> 01:00:41,230
I think it's reversed, sorry.

1397
01:00:41,230 --> 01:00:42,730
N should be the amount of parameters

1398
01:00:42,730 --> 01:00:46,010
and D should be the amount of data.

1399
01:00:46,010 --> 01:00:49,250
And so tokens per parameters decreases with C.

1400
01:00:49,250 --> 01:00:51,490
So as you increase the compute amounts,

1401
01:00:51,490 --> 01:00:53,450
this prescription by Kaplan tells

1402
01:00:53,450 --> 01:00:56,010
you to train bigger and bigger and bigger models.

1403
01:00:56,010 --> 01:00:58,303
And so if you were around in the days of GPT-3,

1404
01:00:58,303 --> 01:00:59,970
it kind of shocks me to think that there

1405
01:00:59,970 --> 01:01:02,890
are people who were not, you would

1406
01:01:02,890 --> 01:01:05,410
know that there was a period where everyone was

1407
01:01:05,410 --> 01:01:06,730
training these gigantic models.

1408
01:01:06,730 --> 01:01:09,370
Like hundreds of billions of parameters

1409
01:01:09,370 --> 01:01:12,770
of models, trillion-parameter dense models, what have you.

1410
01:01:12,770 --> 01:01:14,570
And part of that was driven by this.

1411
01:01:14,570 --> 01:01:17,110
They saw GPT-3, they saw these Kaplan predictions,

1412
01:01:17,110 --> 01:01:19,570
and they said, "Wow, I'm going to do my big training runs

1413
01:01:19,570 --> 01:01:24,520
on these gigantic model size models."

1414
01:01:24,520 --> 01:01:29,943
But a few years later, in 2022, some folks at DeepMind, Hoffman

1415
01:01:29,943 --> 01:01:31,860
et al., came in and they said, well, actually,

1416
01:01:31,860 --> 01:01:33,793
these predictions are all terribly off.

1417
01:01:33,793 --> 01:01:35,960
You've been training these giant models, these three

1418
01:01:35,960 --> 01:01:39,060
stars over here, but actually, those are way too big.

1419
01:01:39,060 --> 01:01:40,560
What we should be training instead

1420
01:01:40,560 --> 01:01:45,000
is a very different trend, this line on the blue over here,

1421
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:47,440
and we should be training this teal star, which

1422
01:01:47,440 --> 01:01:48,640
is a much better model.

1423
01:01:48,640 --> 01:01:51,480
So they argued that we should be training much smaller models

1424
01:01:51,480 --> 01:01:53,160
relative to this.

1425
01:01:53,160 --> 01:01:55,960
And I think, of course, many of you

1426
01:01:55,960 --> 01:01:57,953
or all of you probably the Chinchilla paper,

1427
01:01:57,953 --> 01:02:00,160
you know what the token multipliers are.

1428
01:02:00,160 --> 01:02:02,080
It's 20 tokens to every parameter.

1429
01:02:02,080 --> 01:02:04,600
But I think this is useful to go in detail

1430
01:02:04,600 --> 01:02:08,420
because the Chinchilla-Kaplan disagreement and all of this

1431
01:02:08,420 --> 01:02:10,680
tells us something important about how we should

1432
01:02:10,680 --> 01:02:11,972
be thinking about scaling laws.

1433
01:02:11,972 --> 01:02:14,013
It's not just something where you turn the crank,

1434
01:02:14,013 --> 01:02:15,200
and you get the answer.

1435
01:02:15,200 --> 01:02:17,660
It's something that's actually careful and sensitive,

1436
01:02:17,660 --> 01:02:21,887
and we have to respect the process.

1437
01:02:21,887 --> 01:02:23,970
So I'm going to walk through the Chinchilla paper.

1438
01:02:23,970 --> 01:02:25,650
And after I walk through the Chinchilla paper,

1439
01:02:25,650 --> 01:02:27,910
I'm going to talk about why the Kaplan and Chinchilla

1440
01:02:27,910 --> 01:02:30,810
papers disagree and what that means for you.

1441
01:02:30,810 --> 01:02:34,230
So the Chinchilla author suggests three different ways

1442
01:02:34,230 --> 01:02:35,370
of fitting scaling laws.

1443
01:02:35,370 --> 01:02:39,110
I really like this because it's a way of robustifying yourself

1444
01:02:39,110 --> 01:02:41,750
to modeling assumptions you may have made.

1445
01:02:41,750 --> 01:02:44,470
And each of these actually is quite a different approach

1446
01:02:44,470 --> 01:02:49,710
to estimating the trade-off between model size and data.

1447
01:02:49,710 --> 01:02:53,550
And minus method 3, which does differ a little bit,

1448
01:02:53,550 --> 01:02:54,770
they mostly agree.

1449
01:02:54,770 --> 01:02:57,990
All 1 and 2 both agree that the coefficients

1450
01:02:57,990 --> 01:03:01,310
for data and for model size are roughly the same.

1451
01:03:01,310 --> 01:03:04,030
0.5, 0.5, 0.49, 0.51.

1452
01:03:04,030 --> 01:03:08,270
Method 3 does differ, 0.46 and 0.54, but we'll talk about why

1453
01:03:08,270 --> 01:03:09,130
later.

1454
01:03:09,130 --> 01:03:11,550
Kaplan et al., of course, have a very big difference

1455
01:03:11,550 --> 01:03:14,350
between the two, which is why they were training big models

1456
01:03:14,350 --> 01:03:17,350
at big, big compute sizes.

1457
01:03:17,350 --> 01:03:19,940
Method number 1 from Chinchilla is

1458
01:03:19,940 --> 01:03:22,200
what I might call the lower envelope method.

1459
01:03:22,200 --> 01:03:25,940
And this is the method that was also being used partially

1460
01:03:25,940 --> 01:03:29,500
by Kaplan to try to figure out what the right scaling was.

1461
01:03:29,500 --> 01:03:32,400
And so what you do is you take all of your training runs.

1462
01:03:32,400 --> 01:03:36,042
So each one of these colored lines is a training curve.

1463
01:03:36,042 --> 01:03:37,500
And what you want to do is you want

1464
01:03:37,500 --> 01:03:40,420
to actually take the lower envelope of these training curves.

1465
01:03:40,420 --> 01:03:43,180
Because a lower envelope point on this training curve,

1466
01:03:43,180 --> 01:03:45,480
that means that for a particular FLOP,

1467
01:03:45,480 --> 01:03:48,900
this is the best loss that I ever achieved for that FLOP

1468
01:03:48,900 --> 01:03:50,040
on any training run.

1469
01:03:50,040 --> 01:03:52,620
So I can take all of the lower envelope

1470
01:03:52,620 --> 01:03:55,640
points at the very bottom here at the high compute regime,

1471
01:03:55,640 --> 01:03:57,420
and then I can plot all those points

1472
01:03:57,420 --> 01:04:01,020
to see how big were the models that each of these FLOP points

1473
01:04:01,020 --> 01:04:01,710
intersected.

1474
01:04:01,710 --> 01:04:03,460
Because each of these points is a training

1475
01:04:03,460 --> 01:04:05,080
run with a model size.

1476
01:04:05,080 --> 01:04:08,160
And if I scatter plot that, I will get a nice linear trend.

1477
01:04:08,160 --> 01:04:11,660
And that will give me one way of estimating the optimal trade-off

1478
01:04:11,660 --> 01:04:13,840
between FLOP to parameters.

1479
01:04:13,840 --> 01:04:16,840
And that gives one answer, which is for their compute budget,

1480
01:04:16,840 --> 01:04:19,580
they should train a 67 billion parameter model.

1481
01:04:19,580 --> 01:04:23,100
This is a nice and simple way, but very tricky defining things

1482
01:04:23,100 --> 01:04:25,500
like what is the lower envelope, and so on and so forth.

1483
01:04:25,500 --> 01:04:28,740
There's definitely some tricky parts.

1484
01:04:28,740 --> 01:04:30,580
Method 2 is IsoFLOP.

1485
01:04:30,580 --> 01:04:32,200
This is now very popular.

1486
01:04:32,200 --> 01:04:34,160
It's also very easy and very robust.

1487
01:04:34,160 --> 01:04:37,140
This is probably my personal favorite method.

1488
01:04:37,140 --> 01:04:39,100
And what you do is, for this, you

1489
01:04:39,100 --> 01:04:41,600
pick a bunch of different FLOPS budgets.

1490
01:04:41,600 --> 01:04:45,340
So I have a different range of FLOPS budgets going in factors

1491
01:04:45,340 --> 01:04:49,300
of 3 in this case or 1, 3, 6.

1492
01:04:49,300 --> 01:04:51,140
And what you do is for each FLOP budget,

1493
01:04:51,140 --> 01:04:53,220
you're going to essentially sweep the space.

1494
01:04:53,220 --> 01:04:54,860
So here I have one variable, which

1495
01:04:54,860 --> 01:04:56,600
is the parameter-to-data trade-off.

1496
01:04:56,600 --> 01:04:58,500
So for each FLOP budget, I'm going

1497
01:04:58,500 --> 01:05:02,240
to sweep over the parameter to data trade-off, fixing my FLOPS.

1498
01:05:02,240 --> 01:05:04,580
So I double my data size, I half my model size,

1499
01:05:04,580 --> 01:05:05,400
so on and so forth.

1500
01:05:05,400 --> 01:05:08,020
And that will generate a sequence of training runs

1501
01:05:08,020 --> 01:05:11,040
whose terminal loss is going to sweep out some of curve.

1502
01:05:11,040 --> 01:05:13,500
So each one of these colored sets of points

1503
01:05:13,500 --> 01:05:15,240
is a fixed FLOP run.

1504
01:05:15,240 --> 01:05:18,110
Each point is sweeping across different parameters,

1505
01:05:18,110 --> 01:05:20,910
and of course, it's implicitly sweeping over data as well.

1506
01:05:20,910 --> 01:05:25,050
Now, the nice thing now is I can take the minimum over each

1507
01:05:25,050 --> 01:05:26,770
of these runs, or even fit a quadratic

1508
01:05:26,770 --> 01:05:28,810
and take the minimum over the quadratics,

1509
01:05:28,810 --> 01:05:30,570
and then I can draw a line through all

1510
01:05:30,570 --> 01:05:32,230
of the bottoms of the quadratics,

1511
01:05:32,230 --> 01:05:34,490
or take the bottom of the quadratics

1512
01:05:34,490 --> 01:05:38,050
and plot them as a function of FLOPS to parameters.

1513
01:05:38,050 --> 01:05:39,787
And that will give me another prediction,

1514
01:05:39,787 --> 01:05:40,870
which agrees very closely.

1515
01:05:40,870 --> 01:05:44,190
These is $63 billion parameters for my compute budget.

1516
01:05:47,503 --> 01:05:50,930
Now, the final approach that we can do

1517
01:05:50,930 --> 01:05:52,790
is actually the maybe most natural one,

1518
01:05:52,790 --> 01:05:56,090
as suggested by the Rosenfeld paper as well as the Kaplan

1519
01:05:56,090 --> 01:06:00,050
paper, because both of them are propose

1520
01:06:00,050 --> 01:06:03,770
a hypothesized functional form for how model

1521
01:06:03,770 --> 01:06:06,050
size 和数据与 loss 相关。

1522
01:06:06,050 --> 01:06:09,810
所以如果你有这样的联合函数形式，你可以做的是

1523
01:06:09,810 --> 01:06:13,450
你可以取一大堆你已经做过的训练运行，

1524
01:06:13,450 --> 01:06:16,600
然后你可以拟合你的 loss landscape，也就是

1525
01:06:16,600 --> 01:06:18,180
你假设的函数形式，

1526
01:06:18,180 --> 01:06:19,560
然后你可以直接做曲线拟合

1527
01:06:19,560 --> 01:06:22,268
来估计你的缩放定律中的系数。

1528
01:06:22,268 --> 01:06:23,800
这是最暴力的方式。

1529
01:06:23,800 --> 01:06:25,520
你有一个假设的关系

1530
01:06:25,520 --> 01:06:28,520
在 loss、数据量和模型大小之间，

1531
01:06:28,520 --> 01:06:31,880
训练一堆模型，做曲线拟合。

1532
01:06:31,880 --> 01:06:35,380
希望这相当直接——你如何做到这一点。

1533
01:06:35,380 --> 01:06:37,800
当然，如果你在用这种方法，你如何进行

1534
01:06:37,800 --> 01:06:39,402
曲线拟合非常重要。

1535
01:06:39,402 --> 01:06:40,860
现在，你在拟合这些曲面，

1536
01:06:40,860 --> 01:06:43,760
有很多变量，这有点棘手。

1537
01:06:43,760 --> 01:06:47,820
这个东西也有一些棘手之处，就像方法 1 一样。

1538
01:06:47,820 --> 01:06:51,000
所以这不像

1539
01:06:51,000 --> 01:06:53,240
也许 FLOPS 那样直接。

1540
01:06:53,240 --> 01:06:56,820
所以现在我们已经讨论了 Chinchilla 做了什么，

1541
01:06:56,820 --> 01:06:59,360
它看起来与

1542
01:06:59,360 --> 01:07:00,220
Kaplan 所做的没有本质区别。

1543
01:07:00,220 --> 01:07:03,080
并不是 Kaplan 做了什么疯狂的事，然后

1544
01:07:03,080 --> 01:07:05,800
Chinchilla 的作者做了更聪明的事，而你

1545
01:07:05,800 --> 01:07:08,400
说：“哇，为什么我们不从一开始就这么做？”

1546
01:07:08,400 --> 01:07:10,520
他们都做了相当合理的事情。

1547
01:07:10,520 --> 01:07:12,620
但如果我们看他们的缩放预测，

1548
01:07:12,620 --> 01:07:14,030
它们非常不同。

1549
01:07:14,030 --> 01:07:18,110
事后看来，我们通常倾向于同意

1550
01:07:18,110 --> 01:07:21,250
Chinchilla 的作者有更多正确的缩放。

1551
01:07:21,250 --> 01:07:24,210
如果你做你自己的 Chinchilla 风格分析，

1552
01:07:24,210 --> 01:07:27,750
你会发现缩放可能更接近 Chinchilla。

1553
01:07:27,750 --> 01:07:30,910
那么，为什么会有这么大的差异，当

1554
01:07:30,910 --> 01:07:34,390
他们都在拟合这些联合缩放定律时？

1555
01:07:34,390 --> 01:07:37,350
好的，所以这是我认为我们

1556
01:07:37,350 --> 01:07:42,210
进入缩放定律是如何制定的这些混乱现实的地方。

1557
01:07:42,210 --> 01:07:45,310
缩放定律真的会变化，取决于

1558
01:07:45,310 --> 01:07:48,230
你实现缩放定律的精确方式，

1559
01:07:48,230 --> 01:07:50,890
你使用的超参数设置，

1560
01:07:50,890 --> 01:07:53,870
甚至 x 轴是什么。

1561
01:07:53,870 --> 01:07:55,640
有一篇非常好的论文。

1562
01:07:55,640 --> 01:07:56,890
哦，不，我把作者遮住了。

1563
01:07:56,890 --> 01:08:02,933
我之后可以把它粘贴到课程的 Slack 里。

1564
01:08:02,933 --> 01:08:05,350
——叫《Resolving Discrepancy to Compute-Optimal Scaling

1565
01:08:05,350 --> 01:08:06,770
of Language Models》。

1566
01:08:06,770 --> 01:08:07,770
Yair 是最后一位作者。

1567
01:08:07,770 --> 01:08:09,750
他曾是这里的学生。

1568
01:08:09,750 --> 01:08:12,590
他们展示——基本上，首先他们

1569
01:08:12,590 --> 01:08:15,110
用大致 Kaplan 的设置

1570
01:08:15,110 --> 01:08:16,689
复现了 Kaplan 的结果。

1571
01:08:16,689 --> 01:08:18,670
然后他们说，好吧，让我们

1572
01:08:18,670 --> 01:08:20,330
改变参数的计数方式。

1573
01:08:20,330 --> 01:08:22,013
这会让曲线稍微移动一点。

1574
01:08:22,013 --> 01:08:23,430
然后他们说，好吧，让我们

1575
01:08:23,430 --> 01:08:25,290
改变学习率预热的方式。

1576
01:08:25,290 --> 01:08:26,870
这会让曲线稍微移动一点。

1577
01:08:26,870 --> 01:08:29,090
然后让我们再稍微调一调优化器。

1578
01:08:29,090 --> 01:08:31,450
然后他们就得到了和 Chinchilla 完全一致的结果。

1579
01:08:31,450 --> 01:08:33,350
所以他们的论点是，这里存在

1580
01:08:33,350 --> 01:08:38,370
一系列看起来非常微小的决定，最终

1581
01:08:38,370 --> 01:08:40,750
在 Kaplan 缩放定律

1582
01:08:40,750 --> 01:08:42,850
和 Chinchilla 缩放定律之间造成了这么大的差距。

1583
01:08:42,850 --> 01:08:46,069
我会解释这些步骤各自

1584
01:08:46,069 --> 01:08:49,229
是什么，并不是说这是最重要的单一因素，

1585
01:08:49,229 --> 01:08:53,069
而是也想向你强调，这里的细节有多么重要。

1586
01:08:53,069 --> 01:08:55,830
所以第一个箭头，我们从 Kaplan

1587
01:08:55,830 --> 01:09:00,010
到蓝线的这一步，就是参数的计数方式。

1588
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:02,375
所以记住，在 Kaplan 中，我说过，好吧，

1589
01:09:02,375 --> 01:09:03,750
如果他们把嵌入参数

1590
01:09:03,750 --> 01:09:05,270
也算进去，就会看到这些奇怪的现象。

1591
01:09:05,270 --> 01:09:07,229
所以他们排除了嵌入参数。

1592
01:09:07,229 --> 01:09:09,899
这通常是可以接受的做法。

1593
01:09:09,899 --> 01:09:12,740
但他们还做了一件真正把他们搞砸的事，

1594
01:09:12,740 --> 01:09:16,380
就是他们也排除了最后一层的参数

1595
01:09:16,380 --> 01:09:18,200
计数，也就是 softmax 那些。

1596
01:09:18,200 --> 01:09:21,220
因为在许多模型中，嵌入层和最后一层

1597
01:09:21,220 --> 01:09:22,580
线性层是互为对偶的。

1598
01:09:22,580 --> 01:09:24,380
它们有相同的形状。

1599
01:09:24,380 --> 01:09:27,720
嵌入矩阵是词表大小乘以隐藏维度。

1600
01:09:27,720 --> 01:09:30,502
输出矩阵是隐藏维度乘以词表大小。

1601
01:09:30,502 --> 01:09:31,960
And because they're the same shape,

1602
01:09:31,960 --> 01:09:34,043
they said, well, let's just exclude both of those.

1603
01:09:34,043 --> 01:09:36,220
It turns out whether you include or exclude

1604
01:09:36,220 --> 01:09:40,700
those has a big material impact on the shape of the scaling law.

1605
01:09:40,700 --> 01:09:44,399
They also found-- this is much more of an oversight,

1606
01:09:44,399 --> 01:09:48,060
more than a genuine difference in opinion,

1607
01:09:48,060 --> 01:09:51,420
that a lot of the models that were being trained in Kaplan

1608
01:09:51,420 --> 01:09:52,359
were very small.

1609
01:09:52,359 --> 01:09:55,100
And in fact, they were so small that they were not actually

1610
01:09:55,100 --> 01:09:58,080
converging by the time that the learning rate warm up was done.

1611
01:09:58,080 --> 01:10:00,280
So their learning rates were set very suboptimally.

1612
01:10:00,280 --> 01:10:03,740
And because of that, their models weren't fully converged.

1613
01:10:03,740 --> 01:10:07,300
And then finally, they found that Kaplan et al. was fixing

1614
01:10:07,300 --> 01:10:09,650
one big batch size, and those big batch sizes

1615
01:10:09,650 --> 01:10:11,670
were suboptimal for the smaller models.

1616
01:10:11,670 --> 01:10:14,170
And therefore, if you tune that correctly

1617
01:10:14,170 --> 01:10:15,890
and you use varying batch sizes, you

1618
01:10:15,890 --> 01:10:20,210
end up getting this thing that agrees exactly with Chinchilla.

1619
01:10:20,210 --> 01:10:21,890
So I think each of these might seem

1620
01:10:21,890 --> 01:10:25,912
like a very minor difference, but if you change up

1621
01:10:25,912 --> 01:10:27,370
these really minor differences, you

1622
01:10:27,370 --> 01:10:29,810
can get big shifts in the scaling law.

1623
01:10:29,810 --> 01:10:32,050
And one thing I like to emphasize

1624
01:10:32,050 --> 01:10:35,530
is that scaling laws are lower bounds in some sense.

1625
01:10:35,530 --> 01:10:37,770
They're kind of saying, if I continue this recipe

1626
01:10:37,770 --> 01:10:41,210
and I scale it up, then this is what I will get.

1627
01:10:41,210 --> 01:10:43,610
But if you're scaling up a recipe that you don't want

1628
01:10:43,610 --> 01:10:45,508
to scale up, your warmup is kind of crazy,

1629
01:10:45,508 --> 01:10:47,050
or your batch sizes are crazy, you're

1630
01:10:47,050 --> 01:10:48,450
going to get bad scaling laws.

1631
01:10:48,450 --> 01:10:54,050
So you want to be as close to the proper full run as possible.

1632
01:10:54,050 --> 01:10:57,450
There's another paper that does another very interesting thing

1633
01:10:57,450 --> 01:11:00,130
to try to study the difference between Kaplan and Chinchilla

1634
01:11:00,130 --> 01:11:01,010
scaling laws.

1635
01:11:01,010 --> 01:11:03,450
It's by Pearce and Song.

1636
01:11:03,450 --> 01:11:06,210
And what they do, and I think this is very clever,

1637
01:11:06,210 --> 01:11:07,620
is they don't train any models.

1638
01:11:07,620 --> 01:11:09,560
They just take the Chinchilla training

1639
01:11:09,560 --> 01:11:12,640
curves or the implied functional form of the Chinchilla training

1640
01:11:12,640 --> 01:11:13,140
curves.

1641
01:11:13,140 --> 01:11:15,500
They just plot them out and they say, well,

1642
01:11:15,500 --> 01:11:17,560
based on these implied training curves, which

1643
01:11:17,560 --> 01:11:20,120
are simulated purely from Chinchilla,

1644
01:11:20,120 --> 01:11:22,400
they generate what the implied training curves will

1645
01:11:22,400 --> 01:11:25,520
be for Kaplan if you ignore the embedding parameters

1646
01:11:25,520 --> 01:11:27,920
and you scale way down, and what happens

1647
01:11:27,920 --> 01:11:30,300
if you use the total parameter count instead.

1648
01:11:30,300 --> 01:11:32,580
And what they find is really two effects.

1649
01:11:32,580 --> 01:11:35,900
One is that Kaplan is operating at a much lower compute scale,

1650
01:11:35,900 --> 01:11:38,260
so they're very sensitive to small changes,

1651
01:11:38,260 --> 01:11:40,600
and actually ignoring the non-embedding parameter

1652
01:11:40,600 --> 01:11:43,720
is a sufficiently big change to mess up the result relative

1653
01:11:43,720 --> 01:11:44,500
to Chinchilla.

1654
01:11:44,500 --> 01:11:48,060
So their argument is slightly different from Yair and others,

1655
01:11:48,060 --> 01:11:49,520
which is that here, actually, it's

1656
01:11:49,520 --> 01:11:53,160
the size of the compute scale plus the slight non-linearity

1657
01:11:53,160 --> 01:11:55,360
induced by non-embedding parameters that's

1658
01:11:55,360 --> 01:11:57,700
causing this disagreement.

1659
01:11:57,700 --> 01:12:01,580
So this is really starting to get into the weeds.

1660
01:12:01,580 --> 01:12:04,520
But I hope you appreciate the importance

1661
01:12:04,520 --> 01:12:06,420
of paying attention to detail.

1662
01:12:06,420 --> 01:12:09,080
And I think a lot of the work that goes on

1663
01:12:09,080 --> 01:12:11,120
in establishing reliable scaling laws

1664
01:12:11,120 --> 01:12:13,320
is about figuring out what the right regime is

1665
01:12:13,320 --> 01:12:17,880
so that you can get reliable scaling across compute scales.

1666
01:12:17,880 --> 01:12:20,680
The final thing, and this is really just more of something

1667
01:12:20,680 --> 01:12:23,760
that I just find amusing more than anything else, which

1668
01:12:23,760 --> 01:12:26,240
is that Chinchilla is this paper that takes down

1669
01:12:26,240 --> 01:12:27,380
the Kaplan paper in a way.

1670
01:12:27,380 --> 01:12:29,720
It's like, oh, you guys were very wrong.

1671
01:12:29,720 --> 01:12:32,020
But there was this one remaining mystery,

1672
01:12:32,020 --> 01:12:34,560
which is that Chinchilla method three never agreed

1673
01:12:34,560 --> 01:12:36,140
with Chinchilla method 1 and 2.

1674
01:12:36,140 --> 01:12:38,140
And the numbers look small.

1675
01:12:38,140 --> 01:12:40,535
And the authors are very unperturbed about this,

1676
01:12:40,535 --> 01:12:42,660
but I think the implications are quite non-trivial.

1677
01:12:42,660 --> 01:12:45,280
If you look at this, methods 1 and 2 basically

1678
01:12:45,280 --> 01:12:48,498
say the amount of parameters and tokens should scale together.

1679
01:12:48,498 --> 01:12:50,540
So there should be a fixed ratio between the two.

1680
01:12:50,540 --> 01:12:53,200
This is where the Chinchilla factor of 20 comes from.

1681
01:12:53,200 --> 01:12:56,660
If you look at number 3, the exponents are different.

1682
01:12:56,660 --> 01:12:58,600
This means if you scale up your compute,

1683
01:12:58,600 --> 01:13:00,220
eventually, actually, in this case,

1684
01:13:00,220 --> 01:13:02,160
you're going to have way more tokens than you

1685
01:13:02,160 --> 01:13:03,542
are going to have parameters.

1686
01:13:03,542 --> 01:13:05,750
So it's going to be actually a very different scaling

1687
01:13:05,750 --> 01:13:09,910
conclusion asymptotically than the other ones.

1688
01:13:09,910 --> 01:13:15,282
And so it turns out that I was certainly not the only one who

1689
01:13:15,282 --> 01:13:18,310
was perturbed about this, except some folks at Epoch AI

1690
01:13:18,310 --> 01:13:20,470
did something about being perturbed.

1691
01:13:20,470 --> 01:13:23,210
They couldn't get either the raw data or the code.

1692
01:13:23,210 --> 01:13:25,510
So what they did was they extracted the data from plots

1693
01:13:25,510 --> 01:13:29,545
in the paper, and they refitted the method 3 regressions.

1694
01:13:29,545 --> 01:13:31,170
They extracted the data from the paper.

1695
01:13:31,170 --> 01:13:35,990
And given that data, they fitted that 2D surface or 3D surface

1696
01:13:35,990 --> 01:13:37,710
that was proposed.

1697
01:13:37,710 --> 01:13:39,590
And then, what they showed is actually

1698
01:13:39,590 --> 01:13:43,570
the original paper must have underfitted the original data.

1699
01:13:43,570 --> 01:13:46,170
And if they redid the curve fitting,

1700
01:13:46,170 --> 01:13:48,910
they could get much lower losses relative to what

1701
01:13:48,910 --> 01:13:52,250
the proposed scaling law in the Chinchilla paper.

1702
01:13:52,250 --> 01:13:55,650
And in fact, if you follow the refitted model,

1703
01:13:55,650 --> 01:13:59,870
you got almost exactly D over 20 is

1704
01:13:59,870 --> 01:14:02,220
20 rule of thumb from the Chinchilla paper,

1705
01:14:02,220 --> 01:14:03,840
which is really a funny conclusion,

1706
01:14:03,840 --> 01:14:05,980
I think, to the Chinchilla paper, where in the end

1707
01:14:05,980 --> 01:14:09,540
it turns out that the authors were more right than they knew.

1708
01:14:09,540 --> 01:14:11,540
Actually, they had made a mistake,

1709
01:14:11,540 --> 01:14:13,780
and that was the only reason why method 3 disagreed

1710
01:14:13,780 --> 01:14:14,840
from methods 1 and 2.

1711
01:14:14,840 --> 01:14:19,220
So it's a nice conclusion to know that the method was

1712
01:14:19,220 --> 01:14:21,580
robust after all.

1713
01:14:21,580 --> 01:14:25,600
OK, so that's the end of the Chinchilla saga in some ways.

1714
01:14:25,600 --> 01:14:29,740
But I will end this section by saying you probably don't

1715
01:14:29,740 --> 01:14:31,340
want the Chinchilla factor.

1716
01:14:31,340 --> 01:14:35,220
At this point, I think this is a pretty common knowledge thing.

1717
01:14:35,220 --> 01:14:37,420
But if you're training a production model,

1718
01:14:37,420 --> 01:14:40,260
you don't really care about saving training compute

1719
01:14:40,260 --> 01:14:41,540
for the most part.

1720
01:14:41,540 --> 01:14:43,460
You can look at all sorts of external surveys

1721
01:14:43,460 --> 01:14:45,660
that people have done of how frontier labs are

1722
01:14:45,660 --> 01:14:46,960
spending their compute.

1723
01:14:46,960 --> 01:14:49,400
Most of the compute is not going into training runs.

1724
01:14:49,400 --> 01:14:53,480
Most of the compute is actually going into R&D and serving.

1725
01:14:53,480 --> 01:14:56,540
And given that, especially for serving, what you want to do

1726
01:14:56,540 --> 01:14:59,100
is you want small models that are capable.

1727
01:14:59,100 --> 01:15:02,790
You do not want big, bloated models that cost a lot to serve,

1728
01:15:02,790 --> 01:15:05,050
even if that minimizes training cost.

1729
01:15:05,050 --> 01:15:08,810
And so what you really want is something that you would call

1730
01:15:08,810 --> 01:15:09,730
"overtrained."

1731
01:15:09,730 --> 01:15:12,003
I put that in quotes because, really, overtraining

1732
01:15:12,003 --> 01:15:12,670
is what we want.

1733
01:15:12,670 --> 01:15:14,410
That's the right amount of training.

1734
01:15:14,410 --> 01:15:16,970
And if you look at all the models that were released

1735
01:15:16,970 --> 01:15:18,790
in the early days, like GPT-3--

1736
01:15:18,790 --> 01:15:21,830
this was under-trained for sure, three tokens per parameter.

1737
01:15:21,830 --> 01:15:25,030
Chinchilla gets to the modern ratio of 20 per parameter.

1738
01:15:25,030 --> 01:15:27,430
And then for a while, people were at 20 per parameter.

1739
01:15:27,430 --> 01:15:28,970
And this is the era, I think, where

1740
01:15:28,970 --> 01:15:31,070
there wasn't that much serving of these models.

1741
01:15:31,070 --> 01:15:34,110
The models were cool, but it wasn't being served at scale.

1742
01:15:34,110 --> 01:15:38,170
And then as you start to move to eras where serving becomes very

1743
01:15:38,170 --> 01:15:39,970
real, now you're overtraining these models,

1744
01:15:39,970 --> 01:15:41,490
and now you've moved into MoEs, and you're

1745
01:15:41,490 --> 01:15:43,990
making all these trade-offs to optimize inference serving.

1746
01:15:43,990 --> 01:15:47,070
So I think Chinchilla is important,

1747
01:15:47,070 --> 01:15:51,310
not because I think the 20-to-1 ratio is the one golden ratio.

1748
01:15:51,310 --> 01:15:54,022
If you're doing stuff for research, it might be.

1749
01:15:54,022 --> 01:15:55,730
But I think the reason why it's important

1750
01:15:55,730 --> 01:15:58,010
is because it tells us quite a bit about how

1751
01:15:58,010 --> 01:15:59,770
we should be fitting scaling laws

1752
01:15:59,770 --> 01:16:02,090
and how to think about scaling laws.

1753
01:16:02,090 --> 01:16:05,770
OK, one final thing I'll end with Chinchilla-wise

1754
01:16:05,770 --> 01:16:08,100
and otherwise for scaling is IsoFLOP

1755
01:16:08,100 --> 01:16:09,850
have been one of the things that, I think,

1756
01:16:09,850 --> 01:16:12,290
has a research tool have endured.

1757
01:16:12,290 --> 01:16:14,290
IsoFLOP are very easy to execute.

1758
01:16:14,290 --> 01:16:16,010
You fix a FLOPS budget, and then you

1759
01:16:16,010 --> 01:16:18,330
sweep over all of the other degrees of freedom

1760
01:16:18,330 --> 01:16:20,450
to see what the surface looks like.

1761
01:16:20,450 --> 01:16:24,610
And this gives you, in general, fairly reliable readings

1762
01:16:24,610 --> 01:16:27,850
of how different parameters vary as a function

1763
01:16:27,850 --> 01:16:31,250
of your free parameters.

1764
01:16:31,250 --> 01:16:34,050
People have done this for-- or actually, one of my students

1765
01:16:34,050 --> 01:16:35,910
did this for diffusion models.

1766
01:16:35,910 --> 01:16:39,770
The MoE study I talked about was an IsoFLOP-style design.

1767
01:16:39,770 --> 01:16:42,030
If you're ever in a situation where you're thinking,

1768
01:16:42,030 --> 01:16:44,830
how am I going to decide all these trade-offs,

1769
01:16:44,830 --> 01:16:47,970
IsoFLOP is always a good default.

1770
01:16:47,970 --> 01:16:52,810
OK, so to wrap up everything for today,

1771
01:16:52,810 --> 01:16:56,490
there's this idea that we have this log-linear regularity

1772
01:16:56,490 --> 01:16:58,800
between the amount of resources we put in

1773
01:16:58,800 --> 01:17:00,140
and the performance we get out.

1774
01:17:00,140 --> 01:17:02,840
And it extends to a wide variety of things, model parameters,

1775
01:17:02,840 --> 01:17:05,880
compute, sparsity level of your MoE.

1776
01:17:05,880 --> 01:17:07,800
And that lets you do a lot of the things

1777
01:17:07,800 --> 01:17:10,740
that I was talking about earlier, as arbitrary choices,

1778
01:17:10,740 --> 01:17:14,400
but do it in a much more evidence-driven way, hopefully.

1779
01:17:14,400 --> 01:17:17,640
And so this allows us to have engineering at scale

1780
01:17:17,640 --> 01:17:22,340
without explicitly relying on doing big model training runs.

1781
01:17:22,340 --> 01:17:24,650
And hopefully, you remember, I think all

1782
01:17:24,650 --> 01:17:25,900
the different components here.

1783
01:17:25,900 --> 01:17:28,100
Data scaling is this very natural object,

1784
01:17:28,100 --> 01:17:29,920
just the exponent is a little bit strange.

1785
01:17:29,920 --> 01:17:32,440
Model scaling allows you to do all these cool engineering

1786
01:17:32,440 --> 01:17:33,020
things.

1787
01:17:33,020 --> 01:17:35,680
And then finally, scaling is this very cool,

1788
01:17:35,680 --> 01:17:37,960
robust predictor, let's call it, for how

1789
01:17:37,960 --> 01:17:41,160
to try to do engineering at a large scale.

1790
01:17:41,160 --> 01:17:41,780
And thanks.

1791
01:17:41,780 --> 01:17:44,180
The next lecture, I think, will be Percy on inference.

1792
01:17:44,180 --> 01:17:47,760
But after that, we will return to more advanced niche scaling

1793
01:17:47,760 --> 01:17:50,460
topics.

